window.MORE_STORIES.push({"id": "r3_ocean2", "lv": 3, "e": "🐙", "t": "The Octopus Who Could Disappear", "req": {"n": "Mika", "k": "ocean", "a": "the ocean"}, "p": ["Deep in a warm coral reef lived an octopus named Inky. Inky had eight bendy arms, three hearts and blue blood. But his best trick was hiding. His skin was covered with tiny color spots, and he could change color in less than one second.", "One morning a hungry shark swam over the reef. The little fish darted into cracks in the coral. Inky had nowhere to go, so he pressed himself flat against a bumpy gray rock. He turned his skin gray and bumpy too. The shark swam right past him. It never saw Inky at all!", "When the shark was gone, Inky turned bright red and orange again. A crab scuttled by, and Inky shot out one long arm to catch his lunch. Scientists who study octopuses say they are some of the smartest animals in the ocean. They can open jars, squeeze through holes the size of a coin, and even learn by watching other octopuses."], "q": [["What is Inky's best trick?", ["He can swim faster than a shark", "He can change his color to hide", "He can breathe out of the water", "He can talk to fish"], 1, "'His best trick was hiding... he could change color in less than one second.'"], ["Why didn't the shark see Inky?", ["Inky swam into a dark cave in the reef", "Inky turned gray and bumpy like the rock", "The shark was fast asleep", "Inky was too small for the shark to notice him"], 1, "He pressed against a gray rock and 'turned his skin gray and bumpy too.'"], ["Which fact about octopuses is in the story?", ["They have three hearts", "They live in rivers and ponds", "They have ten long arms", "They cannot see very well at all"], 0, "The story says Inky 'had eight bendy arms, three hearts and blue blood.'"]]},{"id": "r10_moto", "lv": 10, "e": "🏍️", "t": "The Long Way to Ely", "req": {"n": "Amo", "k": "motorcycle|moto|bike", "a": "motorcycles"}, "p": ["Walt Brenner had owned the 1974 Honda for forty-eight years, and for the last nine of them it had lived under a canvas cover in his garage, between the lawnmower and a box of his late wife's gardening books. His daughter Ruth, who had flown in from Denver to help him sort the house before the move, found him out there on a Saturday morning with the cover folded neatly on the workbench and a battery charger humming on the floor.", "'Dad,' she said, in the careful tone she had been using all week, 'the movers come Tuesday. The place in Reno has one parking space.' He did not look up from the carburetor. 'Then I'd better ride it to Reno,' he said. 'Four hundred miles. Your mother and I did it on this bike in 1979, the long way, through Ely. I'd like to see if the road still goes where I remember.'", "Ruth had arguments ready, and she laid them out one by one: he was seventy-eight; the bike was older than she was; there was a perfectly good moving truck. Walt listened to each of them with the patience of a man who had already decided. When she finished, he wiped his hands on a rag and said, 'You're right about all of it. Come with me.'", "It took two days to get the Honda running and another to find a helmet that fit her. They left before sunrise on a Wednesday, Ruth on the back with her arms around a father she had not hugged, really hugged, since she was a teenager. Highway 50 unrolled ahead of them across the Nevada basins, empty and bright, each valley a copy of the last with the mountains shifted slightly, as if someone had redrawn them from memory.", "They stopped often. Walt pointed out a diner that had become a gas station and a gas station that had become nothing at all. In Ely he found the motel where he and her mother had stayed, now painted a color he called 'regrettable.' He ordered the same breakfast he claimed to have eaten there forty-seven years earlier and declared it worse, which seemed to please him enormously.", "Somewhere past Eureka, with the sun low and gold, the engine coughed, recovered, and coughed again. Walt pulled onto the shoulder. Ruth braced for disappointment, but he was laughing. 'She did this in '79 too,' he said. 'Same spot, near enough. Your mother made me promise to sell it the minute we got home.' He looked at the bike for a long moment. 'I never did.'", "They fixed it with a screwdriver and a borrowed patience, and they reached Reno after dark. On Tuesday the movers found the Honda already parked in the single space, the canvas cover folded on the seat. Ruth flew home a day later than planned. She had not, she realized on the plane, won a single one of her arguments, and she could not remember why she had wanted to."], "q": [["Which choice best describes a central development in the passage?", ["Ruth moves from trying to talk her father out of the ride to sharing it with him.", "Walt decides to sell the motorcycle after it breaks down, keeping the promise he made long ago.", "Ruth convinces Walt to use the moving truck instead of riding the bike.", "Walt learns to repair engines for the first time."], 0, "She arrives with 'arguments ready,' rides along, and at the end cannot remember why she had wanted to win them."], ["Why does Walt want to ride the motorcycle to Reno instead of shipping it?", ["He doesn't trust the movers to handle it carefully", "He wants to retrace a trip he took with his late wife", "He wants to prove to Ruth that he is still young and strong", "The truck has no room"], 1, "'Your mother and I did it on this bike in 1979, the long way, through Ely. I'd like to see if the road still goes where I remember.'"], ["The detail that Walt declared his breakfast 'worse' and was 'pleased' mainly suggests that", ["he regrets making the trip and wishes he had stayed home", "what matters to him is the memory, not the food", "the motel has gone out of business", "Ruth is embarrassed by him and his old stories"], 1, "He is delighted to find things changed because revisiting the place, not the quality of the meal, is the point."], ["What does the engine trouble past Eureka reveal?", ["The bike was not safe to ride and should have gone in the truck", "Walt had never kept his old promise to sell the bike", "Ruth knew how to fix motorcycles better than her father", "They would not reach Reno"], 1, "His wife asked him to sell the bike after the same breakdown in '79, and he admits, 'I never did.'"]]},{"id": "r10_moms", "lv": 10, "e": "🧦", "t": "The Committee", "req": {"n": "Amo", "k": "mother|mom", "a": "funny mothers"}, "p": ["The three mothers of Hollis Lane had not planned to form a committee. It happened the way most things happened on Hollis Lane: someone's child did something inexplicable, and by the end of the week there was a group text with a name. This one was called Operation Sock, and it began with Priya Nair standing in her laundry room holding forty-three single socks and no pairs whatsoever.", "'It's not possible,' she told Dana Kowalski over the fence. 'Statistically. I have three children. If socks went missing at random, I'd have some pairs by accident.' Dana, who had four children and a dog named Receipt, nodded with the grave sympathy of a veteran. 'Ours go missing in pairs,' she said. 'Both of them. Together. Like they're eloping.'", "The third mother, Grace Obi, was a structural engineer and therefore could not let this go. By Thursday she had produced a spreadsheet. By Saturday the spreadsheet had tabs. It tracked every sock that entered the three households, sorted by color, owner, and 'last confirmed sighting.' It also contained a column labeled THEORIES, which included 'dryer portal,' 'dog (Receipt),' and, in Dana's handwriting, 'the children are selling them.'", "Suspicion fell first on Receipt, who had a documented history of eating paper and was found, on Sunday, asleep on a nest of what appeared to be dish towels. A search of the nest recovered two socks, a mitten, and the TV remote Dana had replaced in March. Receipt was placed on probation. The numbers barely moved.", "The breakthrough came from Priya's youngest, Arjun, age six, who wandered into the kitchen during an emergency meeting and asked why everyone was looking at a computer about socks. When the situation was explained to him, he considered it seriously and said, 'Oh. You mean the snow people.'", "The snow people, it emerged, lived in the shared hedge between the three yards. There were eleven of them. They had been built over two winters out of sticks, pinecones, and the only material the children considered sufficiently soft and weatherproof for their faces. Each snow person had two sock eyes. Several had sock noses. One, the tallest, wore a full sock scarf in a pattern Grace recognized, with a small cry, as her husband's good argyles.", "The committee met one final time, in the hedge, in their coats, holding mugs. Nobody spoke for a while. 'We could take them back,' Dana said eventually. Priya looked at the tallest snow person, whose argyle scarf was fluttering handsomely in the wind, and at the row of small, lopsided faces staring out at the street with such complete confidence. 'Grace,' she said, 'is there a column in your spreadsheet for donations?' There was not. By evening, there was."], "q": [["Which choice best describes the overall structure of the passage?", ["A mystery is investigated with increasing seriousness and resolved by an unexpected source.", "A conflict between neighbors grows more bitter until the three mothers finally stop speaking to one another.", "A child is punished after the adults discover what he did.", "A scientific theory about lost socks is proven correct."], 0, "The mothers build spreadsheets and theories, and six-year-old Arjun casually solves it."], ["The humor of the spreadsheet detail comes mainly from", ["the contrast between a serious professional tool and a trivial household problem", "Grace's lack of skill with computers", "the fact that the numbers in the spreadsheet were completely wrong from the very beginning", "Dana's refusal to help"], 0, "A structural engineer builds a tabbed spreadsheet with a THEORIES column to track socks."], ["What happened to the missing socks?", ["The dog ate them", "The dryer shrank them and they fell apart in the lint trap", "The children used them to make faces for snow people in the hedge", "A neighbor down the street was secretly taking them from the clotheslines"], 2, "Each snow person 'had two sock eyes,' and the tallest wore a sock scarf."], ["The ending suggests that the mothers", ["will make the children return every sock to its owner by the end of the week", "decide to let the children keep the socks as a gift", "stop speaking to one another after the long and tiring search", "move the snow people inside the house for safe keeping"], 1, "Priya asks for a 'column for donations,' and 'by evening, there was' one."]]});
