window.MORE_STORIES.push({"id": "r1_n1", "lv": 1, "e": "🐶", "t": "Pip and the Big Bath", "p": ["Pip is a little brown dog. Mom fills the tub. 'Bath time, Pip!' she says. Pip runs out the door. He jumps in a big mud puddle. Splat! Now Pip is brown and black.", "Mom laughs. 'Now you really need a bath!' Pip gets in the tub. The bubbles tickle his nose. Pip sneezes. Achoo! Bubbles fly all over. Now Mom is wet too!"], "q": [["What did Pip do when Mom said it was bath time?", ["He hid under the bed and would not come out.", "He ran out and jumped in the mud.", "He got in the tub.", "He went to sleep on his bed."], 1, "The story says 'Pip runs out the door. He jumps in a big mud puddle.'"], ["Why did Pip sneeze?", ["The bubbles tickled his nose.", "He had a bad cold from the rain.", "The water in the tub was too cold.", "Mom sneezed first and he copied her."], 0, "The story says 'The bubbles tickle his nose. Pip sneezes.'"], ["Who was wet at the end?", ["only Pip, not Mom", "only Mom, not Pip", "both Pip and Mom", "nobody in the house"], 2, "Pip was in the tub, and the story says 'Now Mom is wet too!'"]]},{"id": "r1_n2", "lv": 1, "e": "🐞", "t": "Lily and the Ladybug", "p": ["Lily finds a ladybug on a leaf. It is red with black spots. 'I will count the spots,' says Lily. 'One, two, three...' The bug walks up her arm. It tickles! Lily counts to seven.", "Then the ladybug lifts up its red back. Little wings pop out. Zip! It flies away. 'Bye, bug!' says Lily. 'Come back so I can count the rest!'"], "q": [["What color is the ladybug?", ["blue with white spots", "red with black spots", "yellow with no spots", "all black"], 1, "The story says 'It is red with black spots.'"], ["How many spots did Lily count?", ["three", "ten", "seven", "two"], 2, "The story says 'Lily counts to seven.'"], ["Why did Lily not finish counting?", ["She got very sleepy and went in.", "It began to rain on the leaf.", "Her mom called her to come eat.", "The ladybug flew away."], 3, "Little wings popped out and 'It flies away,' so Lily asks it to come back so she can 'count the rest.'"]]},{"id": "r1_n3", "lv": 1, "e": "🚚", "t": "Grandpa's Dump Truck", "p": ["Grandpa has a big yellow dump truck. It is full of dirt. Beep, beep, beep! The truck backs up. Ben and Rosa jump up and down.", "Grandpa pulls a lever. The back of the truck goes up, up, up. The dirt slides out. Whoosh! Now there is a big hill of dirt.", "Ben gets a shovel. Rosa gets a pail. They dig all day. They make roads and a tunnel. At dinner, they are dirty from head to toes!"], "q": [["What was in the truck?", ["rocks", "water", "dirt", "toys"], 2, "The story says the truck 'is full of dirt.'"], ["What made the dirt slide out?", ["Grandpa pulled a lever and the back went up.", "Ben and Rosa pushed the truck with all their might.", "The wind blew it out.", "The truck tipped over."], 0, "The story says 'Grandpa pulls a lever. The back of the truck goes up, up, up. The dirt slides out.'"], ["What did Ben and Rosa make?", ["a big sandcastle with a flag", "a snowman with a hat", "a tree house in the yard", "roads and a tunnel"], 3, "The story says 'They make roads and a tunnel.'"]]},{"id": "r1_n4", "lv": 1, "e": "🌧️", "t": "The Best Rain Day", "p": ["Today is park day. But look! Rain taps on the window. Drip, drop, drip. 'No park today,' says Dad. Jo makes a sad face.", "Then Dad gets out big blankets. Jo gets the chairs. They make a fort in the living room. They eat crackers in the fort. They read books with a flashlight.", "'This is the best rain day ever!' says Jo."], "q": [["Why could Jo not go to the park?", ["It was raining.", "The park was closed.", "Dad was sick.", "It was night."], 0, "The story says 'Rain taps on the window' and Dad says, 'No park today.'"], ["What did Jo and Dad build?", ["a boat", "a fort", "a snowman", "a birdhouse"], 1, "The story says 'They make a fort in the living room.'"], ["How did Jo feel at the end?", ["sad", "mad", "happy", "scared"], 2, "Jo says, 'This is the best rain day ever!'"]]},{"id": "r1_n5", "lv": 1, "e": "🐚", "t": "A New Shell for Hank", "p": ["Hank is a hermit crab. He lives in a shell. But Hank is getting big. His shell is too small now. It pinches!", "Hank walks on the sand. He finds a shell. It is too big. He finds a shell. It has a hole. Then he finds a round white shell. He slips in. It fits just right!", "Hank leaves his old shell on the sand. Soon a little crab will find it."], "q": [["What was wrong with Hank's old shell?", ["It had a big hole in it.", "It was too small.", "It was much too big.", "It was lost in the sand."], 1, "The story says 'His shell is too small now. It pinches!'"], ["Why did Hank not keep the first shell he found?", ["It was too big.", "It was too small.", "It was wet.", "A fish was in it."], 0, "The story says 'He finds a shell. It is too big.'"], ["Who might use Hank's old shell next?", ["a bird", "a big fish", "Hank again", "a little crab"], 3, "The story says 'Soon a little crab will find it.'"]]},{"id": "r1_n6", "lv": 1, "e": "🚀", "t": "Rocket in a Box", "p": ["Max has a big box. He cuts a round window. He paints it silver. Now it is a rocket! Max puts on a hat. It is his space hat.", "'Three, two, one, blast off!' says Max. He zooms past the stars. He waves at the moon. Then he hears an alien. 'Max! Dinner is ready!'", "Oh. It is not an alien. It is Mom!"], "q": [["What did Max use to make his rocket?", ["a bed", "a big box", "a wagon", "a chair"], 1, "The story says 'Max has a big box' and 'Now it is a rocket!'"], ["Who really called Max for dinner?", ["an alien", "Dad", "Mom", "his sister"], 2, "The story says 'It is not an alien. It is Mom!'"], ["Where was Max really?", ["at home, playing pretend", "on the moon with the stars", "in space with the stars", "at school with his friends"], 0, "His rocket was a box, and Mom called him to dinner, so he was at home pretending."]]},{"id": "r1_n7", "lv": 1, "e": "⚽", "t": "The Pass", "p": ["Ava's team is playing soccer. Ava gets the ball. She kicks it hard. It misses the net. 'Oh no!' says Ava.", "Now Ava has the ball again. Two kids run at her. She sees her friend Leo. No one is near him. Ava kicks the ball to Leo. Leo kicks it in the net. Goal!", "'We did it together!' says Leo."], "q": [["What happened to Ava's first kick?", ["It went in.", "It missed the net.", "It hit Leo.", "It went over a fence."], 1, "The story says 'It misses the net.'"], ["Why did Ava pass the ball to Leo?", ["He asked her for the ball.", "She was too tired to run.", "No one was near him.", "He was the coach of the team."], 2, "The story says 'No one is near him,' so Leo was open."], ["What is the lesson of the story?", ["Always keep the ball.", "Soccer is too hard.", "Kick as hard as you can.", "Teams win by working together."], 3, "Ava passed, Leo scored, and Leo says, 'We did it together!'"]]},{"id": "r1_n8", "lv": 1, "e": "🐱", "t": "Who Took the Socks?", "p": ["Socks are missing at Nora's house. Dad has one blue sock. Nora has one pink sock. Where did the other socks go?", "Nora looks under the bed. No socks. She looks in the tub. No socks. Then she hears a purr. It comes from the closet.", "Nora opens the door. There is Fluff the cat on a big pile of socks! 'Fluff took the socks!' says Nora. Fluff just purrs."], "q": [["What was the problem in the story?", ["The cat was lost.", "Socks were missing.", "Dad was sad.", "It was cold."], 1, "The story begins, 'Socks are missing at Nora's house.'"], ["What clue helped Nora find the socks?", ["She heard a purr.", "She heard a bark.", "She found a note.", "She saw footprints."], 0, "The story says 'she hears a purr. It comes from the closet.'"], ["Where were the socks?", ["under Nora's big bed", "in the bathtub with the toys", "in the closet with Fluff", "in the yard"], 2, "Nora opens the closet and finds 'Fluff the cat on a big pile of socks!'"]]},{"id": "r1_n9", "lv": 1, "e": "🪨", "t": "Show and Tell", "p": ["It is show and tell day. Kim has a toy robot. Jay has a shell from the beach. Then it is Omar's turn. He holds up a gray rock.", "'This is Bob,' says Omar. 'Bob is my pet rock. He never barks. He never bites. He is very good at sitting still.'", "The class laughs. Mrs. Lee laughs too. 'Bob is the best listener in the class!' she says."], "q": [["What did Jay bring?", ["a toy robot", "a gray rock named Bob", "a pet fish in a little bowl", "a shell from the beach"], 3, "The story says 'Jay has a shell from the beach.'"], ["Who is Bob?", ["Omar's big dog", "Omar's pet rock", "the teacher", "a toy robot from home"], 1, "Omar says, 'Bob is my pet rock.'"], ["Why did the class laugh?", ["Omar said funny things about his rock.", "Omar fell down on the floor.", "A big dog ran into the room and barked at them.", "Bob the rock started to sing a song."], 0, "Omar said Bob 'never barks' and is 'very good at sitting still,' which is funny because Bob is a rock."]]},{"id": "r1_n10", "lv": 1, "e": "👶", "t": "Baby Nell", "p": ["Tom has a baby sister. Her name is Nell. Nell can not walk. Nell can not talk. Nell can not play ball. 'Babies are no fun,' says Tom.", "Then Tom makes a funny face. He puffs his cheeks. He wiggles his ears. Nell giggles! Tom does it again. Nell laughs and laughs.", "'Nell can do one thing,' says Tom. 'She can laugh at my jokes!'"], "q": [["Why did Tom think babies are no fun?", ["Nell cried and cried all day and all night long.", "Nell took all of his toys and would not give them back.", "Nell could not walk, talk, or play ball.", "Nell was always asleep in her bed all day."], 2, "The story says 'Nell can not walk. Nell can not talk. Nell can not play ball.'"], ["What made Nell giggle?", ["Tom's funny face", "a silly song", "a yellow toy duck", "the dog barking"], 0, "Tom 'makes a funny face' and then 'Nell giggles!'"], ["How does Tom feel about Nell at the end?", ["He is mad at her.", "He wants a brother instead.", "He is bored.", "He likes her more now."], 3, "Tom finds something fun about Nell: 'She can laugh at my jokes!'"]]},{"id": "r8_n1", "lv": 8, "e": "🦈", "t": "The Hidden Senses of Sharks", "p": ["Imagine trying to find your dinner in dark, murky water where you can see only a few feet ahead. For many sharks, this is an ordinary day. Sharks have a reputation as simple eating machines, but they are better described as some of the most sophisticated sensing machines in the ocean. Over hundreds of millions of years, they have evolved a set of senses so varied that a hunting shark can gather information about its prey long before it ever sees it.", "Smell is often the first clue. A shark's nostrils, called nares, sit on the underside of its snout, and unlike ours, they are used only for smelling, not for breathing. Water flows through them as the shark swims, carrying chemical traces past sensitive tissue. Sharks can detect some substances at extremely low concentrations, and researchers have found that some sharks turn toward whichever nostril picks up a scent first, which helps them steer toward its source. The popular claim that a shark can smell a single drop of blood from miles away is an exaggeration, but its sense of smell is still impressive.", "As a shark closes in, a second system takes over. Running along each side of its body is the lateral line, a canal just beneath the skin lined with tiny hair cells. When a fish struggles or a swimmer kicks, it sends pressure waves through the water. The lateral line senses these disturbances, allowing a shark to 'feel' movement nearby even in total darkness. Many bony fish have a lateral line too, which helps a school of fish turn together in an instant.", "Sharks can also see better than many people assume. Behind the retina of many species lies a mirror-like layer called the tapetum lucidum, which reflects light back through the eye a second time. This gives the eye a second chance to capture dim light, the same trick that makes a cat's eyes glow in a car's headlights.", "The most remarkable sense, however, is one humans do not possess at all. Scattered across a shark's snout are hundreds or even thousands of tiny pores filled with jelly. They lead to organs called the ampullae of Lorenzini, named after the Italian scientist Stefano Lorenzini, who described them in the 1600s. For centuries, no one knew what they did. In the twentieth century, experiments revealed that they detect electric fields. Every living animal produces faint electrical signals when its muscles contract and its heart beats. A flatfish buried under the sand is invisible to the eye, but its body still gives off a tiny electric signal, and a shark passing overhead can sense it and strike with precision.", "Some scientists suspect that the wide, flattened head of the hammerhead shark spreads these sensors over a larger area, like a metal detector sweeping the seafloor. Other researchers are investigating whether sharks might also use electric sensing to detect Earth's magnetic field and navigate across open oceans.", "Understanding how sharks sense the world does more than satisfy curiosity. Engineers have tested devices that give off electric fields to discourage sharks from approaching, and fishing crews are experimenting with ways to keep sharks away from their hooks. The better we understand these animals, the better we can share the ocean with them."], "q": [["What is the central idea of the passage?", ["Sharks are the most dangerous animals in the ocean and attack anything that moves.", "Sharks use several specialized senses to find prey, often before they can see it.", "Sharks have poor eyesight and must rely on their sense of smell alone to hunt.", "Stefano Lorenzini was the first scientist to study fish."], 1, "The first paragraph says sharks have 'a set of senses so varied that a hunting shark can gather information about its prey long before it ever sees it,' and the rest of the passage describes those senses."], ["In paragraph 1, the word 'sophisticated' most nearly means", ["dangerous, fierce, and hungry", "old-fashioned and out of date", "simple, basic, and easy to understand", "complex and highly developed"], 3, "The author contrasts 'simple eating machines' with 'sophisticated sensing machines,' so sophisticated means the opposite of simple: complex and highly developed."], ["How could a shark find a flatfish that is completely hidden under the sand?", ["It smells the sand moving.", "Its tapetum lucidum lets it see straight through the sand to the fish below.", "Its ampullae of Lorenzini detect the fish's faint electric signal.", "It waits for the fish to swim away."], 2, "Paragraph 5 says the hidden flatfish 'still gives off a tiny electric signal, and a shark passing overhead can sense it.'"], ["Why does the author mention the claim about smelling 'a single drop of blood from miles away'?", ["To prove that sharks can smell blood from any distance, no matter how much water is in the way", "To correct a common exaggeration while still showing that sharks have a strong sense of smell", "To explain why sharks breathe through their nostrils", "To show that sharks prefer blood to other food"], 1, "The author calls the claim 'an exaggeration' but adds that the shark's 'sense of smell is still impressive.'"], ["How is the middle of the passage (paragraphs 2 through 5) mainly organized?", ["It describes one sense after another, roughly in the order a shark might use them as it approaches prey.", "It tells the history of shark research in time order, starting in the 1600s and ending with modern experiments.", "It compares sharks with cats in every paragraph.", "It presents a problem and then argues for one solution."], 0, "Smell is 'often the first clue,' then 'as a shark closes in' the lateral line takes over, followed by vision and finally electric sensing."]]},{"id": "r8_n2", "lv": 8, "e": "🧊", "t": "The Ice Storm", "p": ["The ice started as rain. By the time Marcus went to bed, it was ticking against his window like handfuls of thrown sand, and by morning every branch on Birch Street wore a glass sleeve. The weight was too much for the old maple at the corner. Sometime before dawn it split, dragging down a power line, and the whole neighborhood went dark and silent.", "Marcus's family was luckier than most. His grandfather had installed a wood stove in the living room years ago, and his mother had kept a stack of split oak beside the garage ever since, 'just in case.' By nine o'clock the living room was warm, a pot of oatmeal was bubbling, and Marcus's little sister was treating the whole thing like a camping trip.", "'Somebody should check on Mrs. Halvorsen,' his mother said, looking across the street.", "Marcus groaned. Mrs. Halvorsen was eighty-three and had spent the entire previous summer complaining about his basketball. The thump of the ball on the driveway, she said, gave her headaches. Once she had called his hoop 'a public nuisance' in front of the whole block. Still, he pulled on his boots and inched across the glazed street, arms out like a tightrope walker.", "She opened the door wearing two sweaters and a knit hat. Behind her, the house was dim and so cold that he could see her breath. 'I'm perfectly fine,' she said before he had spoken. 'I have blankets. I have candles. I don't need to be rescued.'", "Marcus almost turned around. Then he noticed her hands, which were trembling as she gripped the door frame, and the kitchen faucet behind her, left dripping so the pipes would not freeze. She was not fine. She simply did not want to be anyone's problem.", "He thought for a moment. 'Actually,' he said, 'we kind of need help. Mom's trying to cook on the wood stove, and she's burning everything. Didn't you say you grew up on a farm?' It was only half a lie. His mother really had scorched the oatmeal.", "Mrs. Halvorsen's eyes narrowed, as if she were searching his face for a trick. Then she sniffed. 'Nobody under sixty knows how to manage a wood fire,' she said. 'Give me five minutes to find my good boots.'", "She stayed for three days, until the power trucks finally reached Birch Street. She taught Marcus's mother to bank the coals at night so the fire would last until morning, and she taught his sister a card game with rules no one else could follow. On the second evening, Marcus caught her laughing at one of his jokes, though she pretended it was a cough.", "In April, when the maples were budding again, Marcus dragged his ball out to the driveway. He had missed four free throws in a row when a voice called across the street. Mrs. Halvorsen was sitting on her porch in a lawn chair, a mug in her hand. 'You're letting your elbow stick out,' she said. 'Keep it under the ball.' He tried it. The ball dropped cleanly through the net. 'Better,' she said, and went on watching."], "q": [["Why did Mrs. Halvorsen first say she did not need help?", ["She had a generator and plenty of heat in her house.", "She did not like Marcus's mother and refused to visit.", "She was too proud to want to be anyone's problem.", "She was about to leave for her daughter's house that morning."], 2, "Marcus realizes, 'She was not fine. She simply did not want to be anyone's problem.'"], ["Which detail shows Marcus that Mrs. Halvorsen was NOT fine?", ["Her hands were trembling and her house was cold enough to see her breath.", "She was already wearing her good boots and was ready to go out.", "She had once called his basketball hoop a public nuisance in front of everyone.", "She had plenty of candles."], 0, "The text says the house was 'so cold that he could see her breath' and her hands 'were trembling as she gripped the door frame.'"], ["Why does Marcus ask Mrs. Halvorsen for help instead of offering to help her?", ["He truly did not know how to cook anything on a wood stove.", "His mother had told him exactly what to say to her at the door.", "He wanted her to feel guilty about complaining about his basketball.", "He wanted her to be able to come without feeling rescued."], 3, "She had insisted, 'I don't need to be rescued,' so Marcus gives her a reason to come as the helper rather than the one being helped."], ["What does the phrase 'every branch on Birch Street wore a glass sleeve' describe?", ["Branches that had been wrapped by neighbors", "Branches coated in a clear layer of ice", "Glass ornaments hanging in the trees", "Broken windows scattered under the trees"], 1, "The storm was freezing rain, so the 'glass sleeve' is a metaphor for the clear coating of ice on each branch."], ["What does the final scene suggest?", ["Mrs. Halvorsen still wants Marcus to stop playing basketball, but she has become far too polite to say so directly.", "Marcus has decided to quit basketball.", "Their relationship has changed, and the basketball that once annoyed her is now something she helps with.", "Mrs. Halvorsen is only outside because her power has gone out again after another ice storm."], 2, "Instead of complaining about the noise, she coaches him: 'Keep it under the ball.' Then 'Better,' she says, 'and went on watching.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n3", "lv": 8, "e": "👃", "t": "The Great Stink", "p": ["In the summer of 1858, London smelled so terrible that the people who ran the country could barely stand to go to work. The heat that June was unusual, and it baked the River Thames, which had become the city's largest open sewer. Newspapers named the crisis 'the Great Stink.' It was disgusting, but it would also lead to one of the most important engineering projects of the nineteenth century.", "The problem had been building for decades. London's population had grown rapidly, passing two million by the middle of the century. Many homes had used cesspits, underground pits where waste collected until it was carted away. As flush toilets became popular, they sent far more water and waste into old drains and sewers, most of which emptied straight into the Thames. The same river supplied drinking water to many Londoners.", "Most doctors of the time believed that diseases such as cholera spread through 'miasma,' or bad air rising from rotting matter. The idea was mistaken, but it was reasonable given what people knew. Cholera outbreaks swept through London several times, killing tens of thousands of people. A physician named John Snow suspected that cholera was carried in water instead. During an outbreak in 1854, he mapped the deaths in the Soho district and found that they clustered around a single public water pump on Broad Street. When the pump's handle was removed, the outbreak there was already fading, but Snow's careful evidence became a landmark in the study of disease. Even so, many experts were not persuaded right away.", "What finally forced action was not a theory but a smell. The Houses of Parliament stand on the bank of the Thames, and in June 1858 officials hung curtains soaked in chemicals over the windows, hoping to block the stench. It did not work well. Before the summer was over, Parliament passed a law giving the city's Metropolitan Board of Works the power and money to fix the problem.", "The job went to the board's chief engineer, Joseph Bazalgette. His plan was bold: rather than let hundreds of drains pour into the river in the middle of the city, he would build enormous 'intercepting' sewers running alongside the Thames. These tunnels would catch the waste before it reached the river and carry it, mostly by gravity, to outlets far downstream, east of the city. Pumping stations would lift the flow where the ground was too flat. Some of the new sewers were hidden inside embankments built along the river, which also created new roads and riverside gardens.", "Bazalgette was known for a practical kind of foresight. According to a well-known story, after he calculated how wide the pipes needed to be, he doubled the size, reasoning that the job would be done only once and the city would keep growing. Whether or not every detail of that story is exact, his system went on to serve a far larger population than he had planned for, and much of it is still in use today.", "The results were dramatic. Work continued through the 1860s and into the 1870s, and as more neighborhoods were connected, cholera faded. London's last major cholera outbreak, in 1866, struck mostly in an eastern area that had not yet been linked to the new system, a grim piece of evidence that Snow had been right about water.", "There is an irony in this history. The sewers were built largely to get rid of a smell that officials wrongly believed was deadly. They succeeded in saving lives for a different reason: they kept sewage out of the water people drank. Sometimes a good solution works even when the people who build it have misunderstood the problem."], "q": [["What is the main idea of the passage?", ["John Snow single-handedly ended cholera across all of London by removing the handle from one public water pump in 1854.", "Parliament moved out of London because of the smell.", "A terrible smell pushed London to build a sewer system that ended up saving lives by protecting drinking water.", "Flush toilets were the main cause of disease in the 1800s, so the city banned them after the Great Stink."], 2, "The passage traces how the Great Stink led to Bazalgette's sewers, and the conclusion says they saved lives because 'they kept sewage out of the water people drank.'"], ["As used in paragraph 3, 'miasma' means", ["bad air that people believed caused disease", "a type of water pump used on London streets", "a map of disease cases drawn by John Snow", "a chemical soaked into curtains to block smells"], 0, "The text defines it directly: 'miasma,' or 'bad air rising from rotting matter.'"], ["Why was the 1866 outbreak 'a grim piece of evidence that Snow had been right'?", ["It happened during another unusually hot summer, when the smell rising from the river was just as strong as in 1858.", "It started at the Broad Street pump again.", "It proved that miasma was the true cause of cholera.", "It struck mostly an area not yet connected to the new sewers, where sewage could still reach drinking water."], 3, "Paragraph 7 says the outbreak 'struck mostly in an eastern area that had not yet been linked to the new system.'"], ["What is the irony the author describes in the last paragraph?", ["Bazalgette never lived to see his sewers completed or used by the people of London.", "The sewers were built to remove a smell but saved lives by cleaning the water supply.", "Parliament ignored the smell until winter.", "John Snow opposed building the new sewers because he believed bad air caused cholera."], 1, "The author says the sewers were built 'to get rid of a smell' but 'succeeded in saving lives for a different reason: they kept sewage out of the water people drank.'"], ["Why does the author include the phrase 'Whether or not every detail of that story is exact'?", ["To prove that Bazalgette never actually doubled the pipe sizes and simply invented the whole story himself", "To suggest that Bazalgette was dishonest", "To signal that the story may be partly legend, while the main point about his foresight still holds", "To show that the sewers are no longer in use and have been replaced by a newer system"], 2, "The author calls it 'a well-known story' and then notes its details may not be exact, before stating the fact that the system served 'a far larger population than he had planned for.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n4", "lv": 8, "e": "🏊", "t": "The Anchor Leg", "p": ["Dani Reyes had been disqualified exactly once in her life, and she could still hear the sound it made. It was not a whistle or a buzzer. It was the silence that fell over her teammates last spring when an official walked down the deck, pointed at lane four, and wrote something on a clipboard. Dani had left the starting block a split second before her teammate touched the wall. In a relay, that single mistake erased everyone's work.", "Now it was the league championship, and Coach Barros had put her on the anchor leg of the 400 freestyle relay again. 'You're our strongest finisher,' he told her. 'But fast doesn't count if it isn't legal. Watch the touch, not the clock.'", "The first two legs went almost perfectly. Mei swam a clean opening hundred, and Jordan held the lead through the second. Then Tess dove in.", "Tess was the newest member of the team. She had joined in September barely able to finish a hundred yards without stopping, and she had spent months arriving early to swim extra laps while everyone else was still yawning in the locker room. She was good now, but she was not yet great, and by the final turn of her leg, two swimmers had caught her. Dani, standing on the block, felt her stomach twist. The girl in lane three was already a full body length ahead, and the girl in lane five was pulling even.", "Every instinct told Dani to go early. If she could steal even a fraction of a second, she might catch them. Her toes curled over the edge of the block. Her arms swung back. Tess's fingertips stretched toward the wall, still a foot away, and Dani's weight began to tip forward.", "Watch the touch, not the clock.", "She held. It felt like standing still in a burning building. Tess's hand slapped the wall, and only then did Dani launch, flat and long, into the water.", "The next hundred yards were a blur of bubbles and burning muscles. At the first wall she could see the lane-five swimmer's feet. At the second she was even with her. By the final length, lane three was right beside her, and Dani pulled with everything she had left, her lungs screaming, her vision narrowing to the black line and the wall rushing toward her.", "She touched and spun toward the scoreboard. Second place, by eight hundredths of a second. Beneath the result, a small flashing symbol marked a new team record.", "Tess was waiting at the edge of the pool, her eyes red. 'I'm sorry,' she said. 'I lost us the race.'", "Dani hauled herself out of the water and thought about what to say. The truth was more complicated than winning or losing. Tess had swum her fastest hundred ever. So had Dani. And the time on the board belonged to all four of them, because this time no official was walking down the deck.", "'You didn't lose anything,' Dani said, still breathing hard. 'You gave me somebody to chase.'"], "q": [["Why was Dani's team disqualified the previous spring?", ["She swam in the wrong lane during the final leg of the race.", "She left the block before her teammate touched the wall.", "She missed a turn during her leg and had to swim back to the wall.", "Her whole team arrived late to the meet and missed the start of the race."], 1, "Paragraph 1 says 'Dani had left the starting block a split second before her teammate touched the wall.'"], ["What does the comparison 'It felt like standing still in a burning building' suggest?", ["Dani was worried that the pool water was far too warm for a fast race.", "Dani wanted to quit the team right then and walk away.", "Dani was angry at Tess.", "Waiting was extremely hard because every instinct told her to move."], 3, "The previous paragraph says 'Every instinct told Dani to go early,' so holding still felt as unnatural as staying in a burning building."], ["Which word best describes Tess, based on the story?", ["hardworking", "lazy", "overconfident", "careless"], 0, "Tess spent 'months arriving early to swim extra laps' and swam 'her fastest hundred ever.'"], ["Why does the author place 'Watch the touch, not the clock' in a paragraph by itself?", ["To show that the coach shouted it loudly from the stands just as Tess reached the final turn of her leg", "To introduce a new character", "To show Dani remembering her coach's advice at the key moment, making it the turning point", "To explain the rules of relay swimming in detail for readers who have never seen a race"], 2, "The line repeats Coach Barros's words exactly when Dani is about to leave early, and the next paragraph begins, 'She held.'"], ["Which statement best expresses the theme of the story?", ["Winning first place is the only thing that matters, no matter how a team gets there in the end.", "Doing things the right way and valuing a team's shared effort matter more than simply winning.", "The fastest swimmer should always swim the first leg of a relay so that the team gets an early lead.", "Newer team members should not be allowed to swim in championships until they have become truly great."], 1, "Dani says the time 'belonged to all four of them, because this time no official was walking down the deck,' and she tells Tess, 'You didn't lose anything.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n5", "lv": 8, "e": "🛒", "t": "Lines in the Sand", "p": ["The next time you watch a cashier slide a box of cereal across a scanner, listen for the beep. That tiny sound is the result of a problem that took more than twenty-five years to solve, an idea sketched in beach sand, and an agreement among companies that usually competed with one another.", "The story begins in 1948 in Philadelphia. Bernard Silver, a graduate student at the Drexel Institute of Technology, overheard the president of a local grocery chain asking one of the school's deans for help. Checkout lines were slow, and clerks had to key in every price by hand, which led to mistakes. The executive wanted a way to record products automatically. The dean was not interested, but Silver was. He shared the problem with his friend Norman Joseph Woodland, a fellow student with a talent for tinkering.", "Woodland became so absorbed in the challenge that he left school for a while and went to stay in Florida, where he could think without distraction. One day, sitting on the beach, he began thinking about Morse code, the system of dots and dashes he had learned as a Boy Scout. Almost without meaning to, he pushed his fingers into the sand and pulled them toward himself, stretching the dots and dashes into long lines, some thick and some thin. A pattern of lines, he realized, could store information just as dots and dashes did, and a machine might be able to read it.", "Woodland and Silver developed the idea together and filed a patent application in 1949; the patent was granted in 1952. Their original design was not the row of stripes we know today but a set of rings, like a target, so that it could be read from any direction. There was just one problem: the technology to read it cheaply did not exist. Their early reader relied on an extremely bright light bulb that gave off a great deal of heat, and the equipment was far too large and expensive for a grocery store. The idea was clever, but it had arrived too early.", "Two developments changed that. The first was the laser, invented in 1960, which could produce a narrow, intense beam of light ideal for sweeping across a code. The second was the growth of affordable computers that could look up a product's price in an instant. By the early 1970s, the grocery industry was ready. A committee of grocery and food companies agreed to adopt a single standard, the Universal Product Code, or UPC, so that every product from every manufacturer could be read by every store's scanners. The committee chose a striped design proposed by IBM, where Woodland was then working.", "On June 26, 1974, at a supermarket in Troy, Ohio, a cashier scanned the first product marked with a UPC barcode: a pack of chewing gum. That pack is now part of the collection of the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History.", "Stores were slow to buy scanners at first, and some shoppers worried that prices would no longer be marked on items. Within about a decade, however, barcodes were everywhere. They sped up checkout lines, and they also changed how stores did business. Because every sale was now recorded automatically, stores knew exactly which products were selling and when to reorder. Barcodes soon spread to warehouses, libraries, hospitals, and airline baggage tags.", "Woodland never grew rich from the barcode; the original patent expired before scanners became common. Still, he lived to see his lines in the sand printed on billions of objects. His story is a reminder that a brilliant idea is sometimes only the beginning, and that its success can depend on timing, technology, and cooperation as much as on the inventor's imagination."], "q": [["What is the main idea of the passage?", ["Woodland became very rich by inventing the barcode and then selling his patent to IBM and to the grocery chains.", "Chewing gum was the most popular product of the 1970s.", "Lasers were invented to read barcodes.", "The barcode began as a clever idea but succeeded only when technology and cooperation caught up with it."], 3, "The last paragraph says an idea's success 'can depend on timing, technology, and cooperation as much as on the inventor's imagination.'"], ["What gave Woodland the idea for the barcode's lines?", ["Morse code's dots and dashes", "The stripes on a zebra he saw at the zoo", "Lines on notebook paper in his class", "A grocery store's printed price tags"], 0, "Paragraph 3 says he was 'thinking about Morse code' and stretched 'the dots and dashes into long lines.'"], ["In paragraph 3, the word 'absorbed' most nearly means", ["soaked up like water", "deeply focused", "confused", "bored"], 1, "Woodland was 'so absorbed in the challenge' that he left school to 'think without distraction,' showing he was deeply focused on it."], ["Why does the author say the idea 'had arrived too early'?", ["Grocery stores with checkout lines and cashiers had not been invented yet.", "Silver and Woodland could not agree with each other on a design for the reader.", "The equipment needed to read barcodes cheaply did not yet exist.", "The patent was rejected in 1949 and had to be filed all over again years later."], 2, "Paragraph 4 says 'the technology to read it cheaply did not exist' and the equipment was 'far too large and expensive.'"], ["Why was the companies' agreement on a single UPC standard important?", ["It let every store's scanners read every manufacturer's products.", "It made chewing gum cheaper.", "It gave Woodland the full profits from his patent.", "It required shoppers to scan their own groceries."], 0, "Paragraph 5 says the standard meant 'every product from every manufacturer could be read by every store's scanners.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n6", "lv": 8, "e": "🍄", "t": "The Network Beneath the Forest", "p": ["Walk through a forest and you see trees, ferns, and perhaps a mushroom or two pushing up through the fallen leaves. What you do not see may be just as important. Beneath the soil, threads of fungus far thinner than a human hair wind through the dirt and wrap around tree roots. These partnerships between plants and fungi are so common that most plants on land depend on them.", "The partnerships are called mycorrhizae, from Greek words meaning 'fungus' and 'root.' A mushroom is only the small, visible reproductive part of a much larger organism. The main body of the fungus is a web of threads called hyphae that spreads through the soil. Because hyphae are so thin and so numerous, they can reach into tiny spaces and absorb water and minerals, such as phosphorus and nitrogen, far more effectively than roots can on their own.", "The relationship is a trade. The fungus passes water and nutrients to the tree. In return, the tree, which makes sugars through photosynthesis, sends some of those sugars down to the fungus, which cannot make its own food. Scientists call a relationship like this mutualism, because both partners benefit. Fossil evidence shows that partnerships between plants and fungi are extremely old, and many researchers believe they helped the earliest plants survive when they first spread onto land.", "Because one fungus can connect to more than one tree, and one tree can connect to many fungi, the result can be a network linking plants across the forest floor. In the 1990s, researchers reported evidence that carbon could move from one tree to another through these fungal links. A catchy nickname soon followed: the 'wood wide web.'", "Over the next two decades, the idea grew into a captivating story. Books, articles, and documentaries described forests where old 'mother trees' fed their seedlings through the network, where trees warned one another of insect attacks, and where the whole forest behaved almost like a cooperative community. The story spread widely, partly because it was so appealing.", "In recent years, however, some scientists have taken a closer look. After reviewing the published research, a group of researchers argued in 2023 that the evidence for several of the most famous claims was weaker than popular accounts suggested. They pointed out that it can be very difficult to prove that a substance traveled through a fungal network rather than through the soil or some other path, and that some field studies had produced mixed results. These scientists did not deny that mycorrhizal networks exist or that fungal partnerships matter. Instead, they cautioned that the most dramatic conclusions had run ahead of the data.", "Other researchers responded that the networks still deserve serious study, and many questions remain open. How much, if anything, do trees actually share through fungal links? Does that sharing matter enough to affect which seedlings survive? Scientists are designing new experiments to find out.", "This debate is not a sign that science has failed. It is how science is supposed to work: an exciting idea is proposed, tested, questioned, and refined. For readers, the lesson reaches well beyond forests. When a story about nature seems almost too perfect, it is worth asking what the evidence actually shows."], "q": [["What is the author's main purpose in this passage?", ["To persuade readers that trees can talk to each other and that 'mother trees' feed their seedlings through the soil", "To explain how trees and fungi work together and to show how scientists are debating claims about forest networks", "To teach readers how to identify edible mushrooms", "To argue that fungi are harmful to forests"], 1, "The first half explains the tree-fungus trade, and the second half describes how some scientists argued 'the most dramatic conclusions had run ahead of the data.'"], ["Based on paragraph 3, 'mutualism' is a relationship in which", ["both partners benefit", "one partner harms the other", "neither partner is affected", "the partners compete for food"], 0, "The text says, 'Scientists call a relationship like this mutualism, because both partners benefit.'"], ["What does the tree give the fungus in exchange for water and nutrients?", ["phosphorus", "nitrogen", "hyphae", "sugars"], 3, "Paragraph 3 says the tree 'sends some of those sugars down to the fungus, which cannot make its own food.'"], ["Why did the researchers who reviewed the evidence in 2023 question some famous claims?", ["They discovered that mycorrhizal networks do not exist at all and that fungi never actually connect to the roots of trees.", "They found that mushrooms are not part of fungi.", "It is hard to prove a substance traveled through a fungal network, and some field studies had mixed results.", "They believed trees make their own nitrogen."], 2, "Paragraph 6 says 'it can be very difficult to prove that a substance traveled through a fungal network' and 'some field studies had produced mixed results.'"], ["How does the author view the scientific debate described in the passage?", ["As a healthy, normal part of how science works", "As proof that scientists cannot be trusted to tell the truth", "As a waste of time that should end as soon as possible", "As a reason to stop studying forests and fungi altogether"], 0, "The last paragraph says, 'This debate is not a sign that science has failed. It is how science is supposed to work.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n7", "lv": 8, "e": "🔬", "t": "The Toughest Animal You've Never Seen", "p": ["If you scoop up a clump of damp moss from a sidewalk crack and look at it under a microscope, you may meet one of the most astonishing animals on Earth. It is plump, has eight stubby legs tipped with tiny claws, and lumbers along like a miniature bear. In fact, its common name is 'water bear.' Scientists call it a tardigrade, from Latin words meaning 'slow stepper.' Most are less than a millimeter long, about the size of a grain of sand or smaller.", "Tardigrades were first described in the 1770s, and more than a thousand species have been identified since. They live almost everywhere: in moss and lichen, in soil, in ponds, in the deep sea, and high on mountains. What has made them famous, though, is not where they live but what they can survive.", "Like all animals, tardigrades need water. An active water bear lives inside a thin film of moisture clinging to moss or soil. When that moisture dries up, a tardigrade does not simply die. Instead, it pulls in its legs and head, curls into a shriveled, barrel-shaped form called a tun, and loses nearly all the water in its body. Its metabolism, the set of chemical processes that keep it alive, slows so much that it becomes almost impossible to measure. This state is called cryptobiosis, meaning 'hidden life.' When water returns, the tardigrade can swell back to its normal shape and walk away, sometimes after years.", "In the tun state, tardigrades have survived conditions that would destroy most living things. In laboratory experiments, some have endured temperatures close to absolute zero, brief heating well above the boiling point of water, crushing pressures, and doses of radiation hundreds of times greater than what would kill a human. In 2007, scientists sent dried tardigrades into orbit aboard a satellite and exposed them to the vacuum of space. After the animals returned to Earth and were given water, many revived, and some even produced healthy offspring.", "How do they do it? Scientists are still piecing together the answer, but they have found several tools. As a tardigrade dries out, it produces special proteins that appear to form a glass-like gel inside its cells, holding delicate structures in place until water returns. One species makes a protein that seems to shield its DNA from damage. When researchers added the gene for that protein to human cells growing in a laboratory, the cells suffered less damage from X-rays.", "It is tempting to call tardigrades indestructible, but that would be misleading. Most of their extreme survival happens only in the tun state. An active, walking tardigrade is fairly delicate and can die from conditions far milder than those it survives while dried out. Their strategy is not to fight harsh conditions head-on but to wait them out, essentially pausing life until the world becomes livable again.", "That strategy has captured the imagination of scientists in many fields. Some researchers hope that understanding tardigrade proteins might one day help preserve medicines and vaccines without refrigeration, or help protect cells from radiation. The humble water bear, hidden in a patch of moss, may still have lessons to teach."], "q": [["What is the passage mostly about?", ["How to find moss under a microscope in a sidewalk crack", "How tardigrades survive extreme conditions and why scientists study them", "Why tardigrades are the largest and strongest animals that live in moss and soil", "How astronauts survive in the vacuum of space with special suits"], 1, "Most of the passage describes 'what they can survive' and how, and the last paragraph explains why researchers are interested."], ["What does 'cryptobiosis' describe?", ["A state in which an animal's life processes slow almost to a stop", "A type of damp moss where most tardigrades like to live and find food", "A special protein that tardigrades make to protect their DNA from X-rays", "The slow, lumbering way that tardigrades walk"], 0, "Paragraph 3 says metabolism 'slows so much that it becomes almost impossible to measure. This state is called cryptobiosis.'"], ["What did the 2007 experiment show?", ["Tardigrades can live in space while active and walking around, without drying out first.", "Tardigrades grow larger in space.", "Dried tardigrades exposed to space could revive, and some later had healthy offspring.", "Tardigrades cannot survive without moss."], 2, "Paragraph 4 says 'many revived, and some even produced healthy offspring.'"], ["Why does the author say calling tardigrades 'indestructible' would be misleading?", ["Tardigrades cannot survive very cold temperatures, even when they are dried out and curled up into a tun.", "Only one species of tardigrade is tough.", "Tardigrades are easily eaten by bears.", "Their extreme survival mostly happens in the tun state, while active tardigrades are fairly delicate."], 3, "Paragraph 6 says 'Most of their extreme survival happens only in the tun state. An active, walking tardigrade is fairly delicate.'"], ["What is the purpose of the final paragraph?", ["To explain how research on tardigrades might someday help people", "To describe where tardigrades were first discovered", "To argue that tardigrades should be protected by law", "To compare tardigrades with real bears"], 0, "It says researchers hope tardigrade proteins 'might one day help preserve medicines and vaccines without refrigeration, or help protect cells from radiation.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n8", "lv": 8, "e": "🌱", "t": "The Greenhouse on Deck Four", "p": ["On the colony ship Meridian, every child over twelve was assigned a job, and Ilse Varga was fairly certain hers was the most boring one aboard. While her friends trained in the navigation simulators or helped repair the water recyclers, Ilse spent two hours each afternoon in the greenhouse on Deck Four, measuring bean plants with a ruler and typing the numbers into a tablet.", "'The system tracks everything automatically,' she complained to Chief Botanist Farrow during her second week. 'Light, water, temperature, nutrients. Why does a person need to measure plants by hand?'", "Farrow did not look up from the seedling she was repotting. 'Because the system only knows what we taught it to look for,' she said. 'Plants don't read the reports.'", "Ilse did not understand what that meant, and she was too proud to ask. She kept measuring.", "By the fifth week, she had noticed something odd. The beans on Rack C were growing faster than the beans on every other rack. At first she felt a flicker of excitement, as if she had discovered a secret. But the Rack C plants did not look healthy. Their stems were long and thin, the gaps between their leaves were wider, and the leaves themselves were small and a pale, washed-out green. Several had flopped over against their supports.", "She checked the system. Water on Rack C: normal. Nutrients: normal. Temperature: normal. Lights: operating at full power, sixteen hours a day. Everything on the screen looked perfect except the plants.", "That night, in the ship's library, Ilse read about how plants respond to their surroundings. A plant that is not getting enough light, she learned, often stretches upward quickly, spending its energy on height in an attempt to reach brighter light. Its stem grows long and weak, and its leaves turn pale. Growing fast, it turned out, was not always a sign of doing well.", "The next afternoon she borrowed a handheld light meter from the supply locker and held it under each rack. Racks A, B, and D gave nearly identical readings. Under Rack C, the number was less than half as high.", "Farrow examined the lamps with her. They were old units, installed before the Meridian had even launched, and their light output had been fading slowly for months. But the ship's system measured the electricity each lamp used, not the light it produced. The lamps were still drawing full power, so the system reported them as perfectly fine.", "'If this had spread to the other racks,' Farrow said quietly, 'we would have noticed it at harvest, when the crop came up short. By then it would have been months too late.' She replaced the lamps that evening and asked the engineers to add light sensors to every rack on the deck.", "Ilse still measured beans every afternoon. The job had not changed, but she had. She no longer thought of it as typing numbers. She thought of it as listening to something that could not speak but was always, patiently, telling the truth."], "q": [["What does Farrow mean when she says, 'Plants don't read the reports'?", ["Plants cannot grow in space.", "The reports are written in a language no one understands.", "Ilse should stop reading the system reports.", "A plant's real condition can differ from what the automatic system says."], 3, "Later, 'Everything on the screen looked perfect except the plants,' showing that the reports and the plants did not agree."], ["Why did the ship's system fail to notice the problem on Rack C?", ["It measured the electricity the lamps used, not the light they produced.", "It had been turned off by the engineers to save power during the long night shift.", "It only checked the plants once a year, before each harvest.", "Ilse had accidentally typed the wrong numbers into it during her first few weeks."], 0, "Paragraph 9 says 'the ship's system measured the electricity each lamp used, not the light it produced.'"], ["Which evidence showed that the fast-growing Rack C plants were NOT healthy?", ["They produced far more beans than the plants on any other rack on Deck Four.", "They needed much more water and fertilizer than the plants on the other racks.", "Their stems were long and thin and their leaves were small and pale.", "They were a darker, richer green than any of the other plants."], 2, "Paragraph 5 describes stems that were 'long and thin' and leaves that were 'small and a pale, washed-out green.'"], ["How does Ilse's attitude toward her job change by the end?", ["She asks to be moved to the navigation simulators with her friends.", "She comes to see careful measuring as meaningful work.", "She decides that the automatic system is always right about the plants.", "She stops measuring the beans and lets the system do the work."], 1, "At first she calls it 'the most boring one aboard,' but by the end she thinks of it as 'listening to something that could not speak but was always, patiently, telling the truth.'"], ["Which statement best expresses a theme of the story?", ["Machines are always more reliable than people, so we should trust them completely.", "Fast growth is the best sign of success.", "Children should not be given important jobs on a ship.", "Careful, firsthand observation can catch problems that automatic systems miss."], 3, "Ilse's hand measurements and light meter readings revealed a problem that the system reported as 'perfectly fine.'"]]},{"id": "r8_n9", "lv": 8, "e": "⏱️", "t": "The Clock That Found the Way", "p": ["For most of history, a ship's captain on the open ocean could answer only half of the question 'Where are we?' Figuring out latitude, your position north or south of the equator, was fairly straightforward. By measuring the height of the sun at noon or the angle of the North Star above the horizon, a skilled navigator could calculate it with reasonable accuracy. Longitude, your position east or west, was another matter entirely. Getting it wrong could mean running onto rocks that sailors believed were many miles away.", "In 1707, a fleet of British warships sailing home in bad weather misjudged its position and struck rocks near the Isles of Scilly, off the southwest tip of England. Four ships were lost, along with well over a thousand sailors. It was one of the worst disasters in British naval history, and it added urgency to a problem that had troubled navigators for centuries.", "The science behind the problem was actually well understood. Earth turns a full 360 degrees every 24 hours, which means it rotates 15 degrees each hour. If a navigator knew the exact time at the home port and could compare it with local time on the ship, found by observing when the sun reached its highest point, the difference would reveal how far east or west the ship had traveled. A difference of one hour meant 15 degrees of longitude. The catch was that no clock of the era could keep accurate time at sea. Pendulum clocks, the most precise timekeepers available, were thrown off by a ship's rolling, and clocks of every kind were affected by changes in temperature and humidity. A clock that drifted by just a few minutes could put a ship dozens of miles off course.", "In 1714, the British Parliament passed the Longitude Act, offering a reward of up to 20,000 pounds, a fortune at the time, to anyone who could find a practical way to determine longitude at sea. Many of the leading scientists of the day believed the answer lay in astronomy, by tracking the precise movement of the moon against the stars. That method eventually worked too, but it required complicated calculations that could take hours.", "An unlikely competitor took a different approach. John Harrison was a carpenter from the north of England with no university education, but he had taught himself to build remarkably accurate clocks, some with gears made of wood. Harrison became convinced that a clock could be built to survive conditions at sea. He spent decades on the problem, producing a series of timekeepers now known as H1, H2, H3, and H4. The first three were large, heavy machines full of ingenious devices designed to cancel out the effects of motion and temperature.", "His breakthrough came with H4, which looked more like an oversized pocket watch than a ship's clock. On a test voyage from England to Jamaica that began in 1761, it lost only a few seconds over more than two months at sea, far better than the Longitude Act required.", "Success did not bring Harrison an easy victory. The Board of Longitude, which judged the prize, demanded further trials and detailed explanations, and some members seemed to favor the astronomical method. Harrison, by then elderly, grew bitter over years of disputes. He eventually received substantial payments, including an award from Parliament in 1773 after he appealed to King George III for support, but he was never formally awarded the full prize.", "In the end, the two methods complemented each other. The lunar method gave sailors a way to check their position, while marine chronometers, built on the path Harrison had opened, became cheaper and more common over the following decades. By the 1800s, a chronometer was standard equipment on long ocean voyages. A question that had once cost thousands of lives could finally be answered with a glance at a clock."], "q": [["According to the passage, why was an accurate clock the key to finding longitude?", ["It told sailors when to eat, sleep, and change watch during long voyages.", "It helped navigators measure the exact height of the North Star above the horizon, which revealed how far east or west they had sailed.", "Comparing home-port time with local ship time reveals how far east or west the ship has traveled.", "It predicted when storms would arrive so ships could avoid dangerous rocks."], 2, "Paragraph 3 says comparing the two times means 'the difference would reveal how far east or west the ship had traveled.'"], ["Why does the author describe the 1707 disaster near the Isles of Scilly?", ["To show how deadly the longitude problem could be", "To explain how latitude was measured", "To introduce John Harrison's childhood", "To argue that the navy should stop sailing in winter"], 0, "The disaster cost 'well over a thousand sailors' and 'added urgency to a problem that had troubled navigators for centuries.'"], ["If a ship's local noon happens exactly 2 hours after noon at its home port, how many degrees of longitude has it traveled?", ["2 degrees", "15 degrees", "60 degrees", "30 degrees"], 3, "The text says 'A difference of one hour meant 15 degrees of longitude,' so 2 hours × 15 degrees = 30 degrees."], ["In the last paragraph, the word 'complemented' most nearly means", ["praised each other in public", "worked well together, each adding something the other lacked", "competed fiercely with each other until one of the methods disappeared completely", "were exactly the same in every way"], 1, "The lunar method 'gave sailors a way to check their position' while chronometers became common, so the two methods supported each other."], ["Which detail best explains why Harrison is called 'an unlikely competitor'?", ["He built a clock that looked more like an oversized pocket watch than a ship's clock.", "He sailed to Jamaica in 1761.", "He was a carpenter with no university education, competing against leading scientists.", "He appealed to King George III for support after years of disputes with the Board of Longitude."], 2, "Paragraph 5 describes him as 'a carpenter from the north of England with no university education,' while 'many of the leading scientists of the day' favored astronomy."]]},{"id": "r8_n10", "lv": 8, "e": "🦉", "t": "The Owl Count", "p": ["Noor had been looking forward to the owl survey for weeks. Her cousin Sam had not. He was seventeen, and he was driving her there only because his mother had told him to, loudly, in front of everyone at Sunday dinner.", "'It's going to be three hours of standing in the dark,' he said as they bumped down the dirt road toward the nature preserve. 'Listening to nothing.'", "'Listening to owls,' Noor corrected him.", "'We'll see.'", "The survey leader was a wildlife biologist named Dr. Pell, a small woman in a wool hat who carried a speaker, a clipboard, and a thermos she did not offer to share. She explained the method as they walked to the first stop. At each marked point along the trail, she would play a recording of an owl's call, and then everyone would stand silently for several minutes, listening for a reply. Owls defend their territories, she said, and a resident owl will often answer what it believes is an intruder. Every response, or lack of one, would be written down.", "At the first stop, the speaker crackled with a series of hoots. Then they waited. A breeze moved through the oaks. Far away, a dog barked. Nothing else. Dr. Pell made a note on her clipboard.", "At the second stop, it was the same, and at the third. Sam's phone glowed in his hand until Dr. Pell gave him a look so pointed that he slid it back into his pocket.", "'So we just wasted an hour,' he muttered as they walked on.", "Dr. Pell stopped. 'No,' she said. 'We found out that no screech owls answered at those three points tonight. Ten years ago, there were owls at two of them. If they're gone, somebody needs to know that and start asking why. A zero is still data.'", "Sam didn't answer, but Noor noticed that he walked a little more quietly after that.", "The fourth stop was at the edge of an old orchard. The recording played. Silence. Noor counted her breaths. Twenty. Thirty. She was about to give up when a sound drifted out of the trees: a string of soft, hollow toots, starting slowly and then speeding up, like a ping-pong ball bouncing to a stop on a table.", "'Western screech owl,' Dr. Pell whispered. She was smiling for the first time all night.", "Then a second owl answered from farther away, and the first one called back. For a full minute, the two of them traded calls across the dark orchard while three humans stood absolutely still.", "On the drive home, Sam was quiet for a long time. Finally he said, 'Do you know when the next survey is?'", "Noor pretended to think about it, although she had the date memorized. 'Next month,' she said. 'Why?'", "'No reason,' Sam said. 'Somebody's got to drive you.'"], "q": [["Why does Dr. Pell play recordings of owl calls?", ["To scare all of the owls away from the trail so that hikers can walk through safely at night", "Because owls defend their territories and often answer what sounds like an intruder", "To teach Noor and Sam how to imitate owl calls for the next survey", "To cover up the sound of the wind"], 1, "Dr. Pell explains that 'a resident owl will often answer what it believes is an intruder.'"], ["What does Dr. Pell mean when she says, 'A zero is still data'?", ["Finding no owls is useful information because it may show that something has changed.", "The speaker and survey equipment are broken and must be repaired before the next monthly count.", "Owls are impossible to count at night, so the survey is only a guess.", "Sam should write zeros on every page."], 0, "She explains that owls used to live at two of those points, and 'If they're gone, somebody needs to know that and start asking why.'"], ["In paragraph 7, a look 'so pointed' is best described as a look that is", ["sharp-edged and dangerous to touch, like a needle", "confused and uncertain about what Sam was doing there", "friendly, warm, and encouraging him to keep going", "clearly disapproving and meant to send a message"], 3, "After Dr. Pell's look, Sam 'slid it back into his pocket,' showing that the look clearly told him to put the phone away."], ["How does Sam change over the course of the story?", ["He goes from excited about the survey to disappointed by it.", "He goes from friendly and polite to rude and unkind.", "He goes from bored and doubtful to quietly interested.", "He does not change at all from the beginning to the end."], 2, "At first he expects 'three hours of standing in the dark,' but after hearing the owls he asks, 'Do you know when the next survey is?'"], ["What does Sam's final line, 'Somebody's got to drive you,' most likely suggest?", ["He is annoyed and grumpy that he will have to drive Noor to the preserve all over again.", "He wants to go to the next survey himself but does not want to admit it.", "He thinks Noor is much too young to go to the survey by herself at night.", "His mother has ordered him to drive Noor to every survey this year."], 1, "Sam asked about the next survey on his own, then says 'No reason,' which suggests he is covering up his own interest."]]},{"id": "r9_n1", "lv": 9, "e": "🎣", "t": "The Theory of Uncle Dez", "p": ["Uncle Dez believed that fishing was a science, and like every science it demanded equipment. On the first morning of our trip to Pine Hollow Reservoir, he arrived at the dock carrying three tackle boxes, a thermometer on a string, a laminated chart of the moon's phases, and a spiral notebook labeled FIELD DATA in letters he had obviously traced. I arrived with a rod I had found in my grandfather's garage and a single rubber worm the color of a traffic cone.", "'That,' Uncle Dez said, pointing at the worm, 'is not a lure. That is a cry for help.'", "He lowered the thermometer over the side and read it the way a surgeon reads an X-ray. Fifty-eight degrees at the surface, he announced, which meant the bass would be sluggish and deep, which meant a slow-rolled crankbait in a crawfish pattern, which meant I should watch and learn. He consulted the moon chart. He consulted the wind. He licked a finger and held it up, then wrote something in the notebook that I am fairly sure was just the word 'wind.'", "For two hours I watched and learned. I learned that there are at least eleven shades of crawfish. I learned that a man can change lures fourteen times without catching a fish, and that each change can come with a brand-new explanation. The water was too clear. The water was not clear enough. A cloud had upset the barometric pressure. A heron on the far bank was, in his words, 'spooking the whole cove with its attitude.'", "Meanwhile, I had tied on the orange worm, mostly because it was the only thing I owned, and I was letting it sink beside a sunken log while I ate a granola bar. I was not fishing so much as keeping the worm company. That was when the line twitched, then tightened, then began moving sideways toward the log with a sense of purpose I had never seen in anything that did not have a driver's license.", "The bass was not enormous, but it was not small, and when I finally swung it into the boat it flared its gills at both of us as if we owed it money. Uncle Dez stared at the fish. He stared at the worm hanging from its lip. He stared at me, and I watched several emotions travel across his face and fail to find a place to park.", "'Interesting,' he said at last. He opened FIELD DATA and wrote for a long time.", "I caught two more that morning on the same worm. Uncle Dez caught a branch, which he described as 'structure,' and a sunburn. By noon I expected him to be sulking, but he was not. He had become fascinated. Why the log? Why orange? Was it the slow fall of the worm, or the fact that I had barely moved it? He began asking me questions, real ones, and writing down my answers as though I were a scientist too.", "On the drive home he admitted, in the careful voice of a man handing over his car keys, that all his equipment might have been a way of feeling in control of something that was mostly patience and luck. Then he asked if I would show him how I had rigged the worm. I told him I had no idea; I had just tied knots until it stopped coming undone.", "'That,' he said, nodding slowly, 'is also a method.' The next weekend he showed up at the dock with one tackle box, one rod, and a bag of orange worms. The notebook came too. Some things you do not give up all at once."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses a central idea of the story?", ["Expensive equipment always produces better results than simple gear, as long as it is used correctly.", "Being willing to learn from surprising results can matter more than having an elaborate plan.", "Fishing success depends mainly on reading the weather, the water temperature, and the moon correctly.", "Young people are naturally better at outdoor sports than adults, who overthink every decision."], 1, "Uncle Dez stops making excuses, starts asking the narrator 'real' questions, and returns the next week with orange worms."], ["The list of Uncle Dez's explanations in the fourth paragraph (the water too clear, a heron with an 'attitude') mainly serves to", ["prove that the reservoir was simply a poor place to fish on that particular morning", "show that the heron on the far bank really was scaring the fish away from the cove", "humorously suggest that he is making excuses rather than finding real causes", "explain the scientific factors that control bass behavior in cold water"], 2, "The explanations contradict each other ('too clear... not clear enough') and end with a heron's 'attitude,' which is played for laughs."], ["What does the narrator suggest by saying Uncle Dez's emotions 'fail to find a place to park'?", ["He is about to drive home in frustration and end the trip early.", "He is secretly pleased and is trying hard to hide his happiness.", "He is worried that the flopping fish will damage his expensive boat.", "He is so surprised that he cannot settle on a single reaction."], 3, "He stares at the fish, the worm, and the narrator, and several emotions cross his face without any one of them settling."], ["Why does Uncle Dez most likely still bring the notebook the next weekend?", ["He still values careful record-keeping even as he changes his methods.", "He plans to prove that the narrator's catch was only luck.", "He needs it to read the moon chart correctly.", "The narrator asked him to write down every fish."], 0, "He has simplified his gear, but the story notes, 'The notebook came too. Some things you do not give up all at once.'"], ["When Uncle Dez calls the branch he caught 'structure,' he is", ["describing exactly how the trays in his three tackle boxes are organized", "giving a technical-sounding name to an embarrassing catch", "explaining why the bass in the cove seemed to like the sunken log so much", "complaining about how poorly the boat he was fishing from had been built"], 1, "He 'caught a branch, which he described as structure,' a fishing term that makes snagging a stick sound deliberate."]]},{"id": "r9_n2", "lv": 9, "e": "✋", "t": "What Pops When You Crack Your Knuckles", "p": ["Almost everyone knows someone who does it: laces the fingers together, pushes the palms outward, and produces a volley of pops that makes the rest of the room wince. The habit has attracted a remarkable amount of scientific attention for something so small, partly because the sound is so satisfying and partly because a real question hides inside it. What, exactly, is making that noise? For most of the twentieth century, the honest answer was that nobody was entirely sure.", "The knuckles in question are synovial joints, the same general type found in the knee and the shoulder. The ends of the two bones are capped with smooth cartilage and wrapped in a capsule of tough tissue. Inside the capsule is synovial fluid, a slippery liquid that lubricates the joint and, like a can of soda, contains dissolved gases. When you bend or pull a finger in the way that produces a crack, you stretch the capsule and rapidly lower the pressure of the fluid inside it.", "Lower the pressure on a liquid quickly enough and something dramatic happens: gas comes out of solution and forms a cavity, a process called cavitation. Researchers largely agreed that cavitation was involved in knuckle cracking. What they argued about was timing. Was the pop the sound of the cavity forming, or the sound of it collapsing? An influential study from the early 1970s argued for collapse, comparing the crack to a bubble bursting. For more than forty years, that explanation appeared in textbooks.", "Then, in 2015, a team of researchers in Canada tried something more direct. They placed a volunteer's finger inside an MRI scanner, attached a cable, and gently pulled until the knuckle cracked, recording the joint in real time. The images showed a dark space appearing within the joint at the instant of the sound, and the space remained visible afterward. If the noise came from a collapsing bubble, the bubble should have vanished. Instead, it seemed to be born with a bang. The researchers compared the event to the sudden separation of two wet surfaces pressed together, like a suction cup pulled off a window.", "The debate did not end there, which is typical of science and slightly annoying for everyone else. A later study used mathematical modeling to argue that the partial collapse of a cavity could produce a sound matching recorded cracks. The two views may not be as far apart as they seem; a cavity might form suddenly and then partly collapse, with each stage contributing something to what we hear. What almost everyone agrees on is the waiting period. After a knuckle cracks, it usually cannot be cracked again for roughly twenty minutes, apparently because the gas needs time to dissolve back into the fluid.", "That leaves the question people actually care about: will it ruin your hands? Generations of children have been warned that knuckle cracking causes arthritis. The evidence so far is reassuring. Studies comparing habitual crackers with non-crackers have generally found no higher rate of arthritis among the crackers. The most memorable evidence comes from a physician who, as an experiment on himself, spent decades cracking the knuckles of only one hand, leaving the other as a comparison. At the end he reported no arthritis in either hand, a result that earned him an Ig Nobel Prize, an award for research that first makes people laugh and then makes them think.", "A single person is hardly a clinical trial, and researchers note that the habit may not be harmless for everyone; if cracking comes with pain or swelling, that is worth a doctor's attention. But for most people, the pop appears to be exactly what it sounds like: a small, noisy event in a fluid-filled space. It is a reminder that even the most ordinary sound in a classroom can contain a genuine scientific puzzle, one that took a magnet the size of a small car to see clearly."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", ["Knuckle cracking is a harmful habit that doctors urge everyone to stop, because it almost always leads to arthritis later in life.", "MRI machines were first built to study the sounds joints make.", "Scientists have investigated what causes the sound of knuckle cracking and have found little evidence that it causes arthritis.", "The 1970s explanation of knuckle cracking has been proven completely correct."], 2, "The passage traces the debate over the sound's cause and says the arthritis evidence 'so far is reassuring.'"], ["The comparison of synovial fluid to 'a can of soda' mainly helps readers understand that", ["the fluid contains dissolved gas that can come out of solution when pressure drops", "the fluid inside joints is sweet and sticky", "joints fizz and bubble constantly throughout the day, even when we are resting", "people who crack their knuckles should avoid drinking carbonated drinks like soda"], 0, "Like soda, the fluid 'contains dissolved gases,' and lowering the pressure lets gas form a cavity."], ["Why did the 2015 MRI study challenge the older explanation?", ["The volunteer reported feeling no pain at all.", "The scanner could not record the joint in real time, so the researchers had to guess what happened.", "It proved that knuckle cracking causes arthritis.", "A cavity appeared at the moment of the crack and remained afterward instead of disappearing."], 3, "'If the noise came from a collapsing bubble, the bubble should have vanished. Instead, it seemed to be born with a bang.'"], ["The author's remark that the ongoing debate is 'typical of science and slightly annoying for everyone else' has a tone that is best described as", ["bitterly critical", "lightly humorous", "deeply confused", "strictly formal"], 1, "The phrase pokes gentle fun at how scientific arguments rarely end neatly."], ["Why does the author add that 'a single person is hardly a clinical trial'?", ["To mock the physician who experimented on himself", "To argue that knuckle cracking does cause arthritis after all", "To acknowledge the limits of one memorable piece of evidence", "To suggest that the Ig Nobel Prize was undeserved"], 2, "After describing the one-hand experiment, the author cautions that one person's result cannot settle the question for everyone."]]},{"id": "r9_n3", "lv": 9, "e": "🧂", "t": "The Rock We Eat", "p": ["Today salt is so cheap that restaurants leave it on the table for free and cities spread it on icy roads by the truckload. It is hard to imagine anyone fighting over it, taxing it into a symbol of tyranny, or marching hundreds of miles to pick it up off a beach. Yet for most of human history, salt was one of the most valuable substances people could possess, and its story runs through economics, politics, and survival itself.", "Part of salt's value is biological. The human body needs sodium to transmit nerve signals, contract muscles, and regulate the balance of fluids. Early humans who ate a great deal of meat got much of the sodium they needed from their food. As people shifted toward farming and diets built around grain, they needed to find salt elsewhere, and so did their livestock. A settlement near a salt spring or a coastal salt marsh had an advantage its neighbors could not easily copy.", "The other part of salt's value was practical. Before refrigeration, preserving food was a constant struggle against time. Salt draws water out of meat and fish, creating conditions in which most spoilage bacteria cannot thrive. Salted fish could feed sailors on long voyages; salted pork could carry a family through winter. In a real sense, salt allowed people to store the surplus of a good season and spend it later, which made it less like a seasoning and more like a savings account.", "Anything that valuable attracts governments. In ancient China, officials of the Han dynasty debated whether the state should control the production of salt and iron, and state salt monopolies became an important source of revenue for centuries. In France, a salt tax known as the gabelle became one of the most resented taxes of the old monarchy, in part because it fell heavily on ordinary households, and it was abolished during the Revolution. Salt is an ideal target for taxation for the same reason it is a harsh one: everyone needs it, so no one can simply stop buying it.", "That harshness made salt a powerful symbol. By 1930, British colonial law in India had long made it illegal for Indians to collect or sell salt outside the government's monopoly, and the salt was taxed. Mohandas Gandhi chose this law as the target of a campaign of nonviolent resistance. He and a group of followers walked roughly 240 miles to the coastal village of Dandi, where Gandhi picked up a lump of natural salt from the shore, breaking the law in front of the world. Thousands followed his example, and many were arrested. The protest did not end British rule on its own, but it drew international attention and showed how a humble substance could carry the weight of a whole political argument.", "Salt has also left traces in our language, though not every story holds up. The Via Salaria, one of ancient Rome's roads, took its name from the salt carried along it. The English word 'salary' comes from the Latin word for salt, and people often claim that Roman soldiers were paid in salt. Historians are more cautious; there is little direct evidence for such payments, and the connection may be looser than the popular story suggests. A good tale, like a good seasoning, can be applied a little too generously.", "Modern industry has made salt plentiful, mined from vast underground deposits and evaporated from seawater on an enormous scale. Its price has collapsed, and its reputation has shifted; today, doctors warn more often about too much salt than too little. But the next time you pass the shaker without a second thought, it is worth remembering that for thousands of years, that small white pile represented food in winter, money in the treasury, and, at least once, the courage to defy an empire."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", ["Salt taxes were the single main cause of the French Revolution and of most other revolutions in history.", "Salt has been biologically, economically, and politically important throughout much of human history.", "Modern people should eat more salt than doctors recommend.", "Gandhi's Salt March ended British rule in India by itself, without any other protests or events."], 1, "The passage covers salt's role in the body, food preservation, taxation, protest, and language."], ["What does the author mean by saying salt was 'more like a savings account'?", ["Salt was deposited in banks for safekeeping, where it slowly grew in value over time.", "People traded salt for interest payments.", "Salt was used mainly by wealthy families.", "Salt let people store food from times of plenty to use during times of scarcity."], 3, "The author explains that salt 'allowed people to store the surplus of a good season and spend it later.'"], ["Why does the author say salt is an ideal target for taxation 'for the same reason it is a harsh one'?", ["Because everyone must buy it, a salt tax is reliable for governments and unavoidable for the poor.", "Because salt is heavy, it is expensive to transport, so governments can easily add a tax at each stop.", "Because salt spoils quickly, it must be taxed before it is used.", "Because only wealthy people used salt in large amounts."], 0, "'Everyone needs it, so no one can simply stop buying it.'"], ["The author's discussion of the word 'salary' mainly shows that the author", ["believes that Roman soldiers were always paid in salt instead of coins", "thinks the history of language is unimportant and not worth studying", "is willing to question a popular story that lacks strong evidence", "wants readers to stop using the word 'salary' because it is misleading"], 2, "The author notes 'there is little direct evidence for such payments' and warns that a good tale can be 'applied a little too generously.'"], ["How is the passage organized?", ["It lists salt mines in order from oldest to newest.", "It opens with salt's modern ordinariness, explains its historical importance, and returns to the present at the end.", "It presents a problem with salty diets and then offers several practical solutions that doctors recommend to their patients.", "It compares China and France and nothing else."], 1, "It begins with cheap table salt, moves through history, and closes with 'the next time you pass the shaker.'"]]},{"id": "r9_n4", "lv": 9, "e": "📝", "t": "Notes in the Lunchbox", "p": ["The notes began in kindergarten and never stopped. Every school day for eleven years, Priya had opened her lunch to find a folded paper napkin with something written on it in her mother's cramped, slanting hand. In the early years they said things like 'You are brave!' and 'Eat the carrots first.' Somewhere around middle school, her mother discovered puns, and there was no going back.", "'Lettuce celebrate your math test.' 'You're one in a melon.' 'I'm not saying I'm proud of you, but I'm grate-ful,' next to a small drawing of a cheese grater that looked more like a tiny prison. The drawings were always worse than the jokes, which was an achievement.", "At sixteen, Priya decided it was time. She raised the subject at dinner, choosing her words the way she had seen diplomats do on the news. She explained that the notes were very sweet, that she appreciated the thought, and that she was now old enough that people at her lunch table could read, which was the problem. Her mother listened, nodding, wearing the expression of someone receiving news she had expected for some time. 'Of course,' she said. 'Consider it done.' Then she passed the rice, and that was that.", "It was that, for exactly two days. On the third day, Priya opened her lunch and felt, before she understood it, that something was missing. The sandwich was there, cut diagonally, the way she liked. The apple was there. The napkin was there, blank and white and folded with a kind of terrible neatness. She stared at it longer than anyone should stare at a napkin.", "'Where's the pun?' said Jonah, across the table.", "Priya looked up. Jonah had been sitting across from her since freshman year, and she had assumed, if she assumed anything, that he spent lunch thinking about basketball. 'You read those?'", "'Everybody reads those,' Jonah said, as if she had asked whether everybody breathed. 'The avocado one was good. Wait, the melon one. Marcus keeps a list.' Two seats down, Marcus raised a hand without looking up from his phone, like a man confirming his attendance at a meeting.", "That afternoon, Priya went looking for a pen in the kitchen junk drawer and found, instead, a rubber band wrapped around a small stack of napkins. Each one was dated. Each one had a pun on it. 'Don't go bacon my heart.' 'You're a smart cookie; that's the way I crumble.' Beneath each joke, her mother had drawn the usual unrecognizable illustration. There were two of them, one for each day since the dinner conversation. Her mother had kept writing them. She simply wasn't sending them anymore.", "Priya stood in the kitchen for a long time with the stack in her hand. She thought about her mother at six in the morning, before her commute, standing at this counter and writing a joke no one would read, then tucking it into a drawer as if saving it for a later that might never come. She thought it was the most embarrassing thing she had ever seen, and she found that her eyes had gone blurry anyway.", "The next morning, her mother found something unusual in her own work bag: a folded paper napkin. On it, in handwriting even worse than her mother's, it said, 'Sorry I was so un-grape-ful. Resume operations.' Below that was a drawing that was either a bunch of grapes or a very worried octopus. Her mother, who had seen a great many bad drawings in her life, decided it was the best one yet."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses a central theme of the story?", ["Teenagers should never feel embarrassed by their parents, no matter what the parents do.", "Puns are the highest form of humor, and parents should use them as often as they possibly can.", "Friends often pretend not to notice each other's lives, even when they are paying close attention.", "Small daily gestures of love can matter more than we realize until they disappear."], 3, "Priya feels that 'something was missing' when the notes stop and is moved to discover her mother kept writing them."], ["The mother's expression of 'someone receiving news she had expected for some time' suggests that she", ["was saddened but not surprised by Priya's request", "was angry that Priya had embarrassed her at dinner", "did not understand what Priya was asking her to do", "planned to ignore the request completely and keep writing"], 0, "She agrees at once ('Of course... Consider it done'), as if she had known this day was coming."], ["The detail of Marcus raising his hand 'like a man confirming his attendance at a meeting' mainly serves to", ["show that Marcus is rude and too distracted by his phone to care about anyone at lunch", "prove that Jonah is lying about the notes to tease Priya", "add humor and show that the notes had become a shared ritual at the table", "explain why Priya wanted the notes to stop in the first place"], 2, "The comic image confirms Jonah's claim that 'everybody reads those' and that Marcus 'keeps a list.'"], ["Why does the stack of napkins in the drawer affect Priya so strongly?", ["It shows that her mother is angry and secretly wants an apology before she will start writing notes again.", "It reveals that her mother kept writing the notes out of love even though no one would read them.", "It proves that Jonah had been stealing her notes.", "It reminds her that she forgot to pack her own lunch."], 1, "She imagines her mother 'writing a joke no one would read, then tucking it into a drawer,' and her eyes go 'blurry.'"], ["The note Priya leaves in her mother's work bag shows that she", ["has decided to embrace the tradition and even take part in it", "wants her mother to stop making her lunch", "is still embarrassed and wants to keep the notes secret", "has learned to draw much better than her mother"], 0, "She writes her own pun and a bad drawing, and tells her mother to 'Resume operations.'"]]},{"id": "r9_n5", "lv": 9, "e": "🔄", "t": "Go Around, Not Through", "p": ["Somewhere in your town there is probably an intersection that everyone complains about. The light stays red too long when no one is coming the other way. Drivers speed up to beat the yellow. Once or twice a year, someone doesn't make it, and there is the familiar scene of flares, broken glass, and a tow truck. Traffic engineers have a proven way to make many intersections like this safer, and it asks drivers to do something Americans have historically resisted: go in a circle.", "First, a clarification. The modern roundabout is not the same as the enormous, chaotic traffic circle some older cities are famous for, where cars enter at speed and jockey for position like pinballs. A modern roundabout is compact. Entering traffic must yield to vehicles already circulating, and curved entrances force drivers to slow down, typically to somewhere between fifteen and twenty-five miles per hour. Those two design features, yielding and slowing, are the whole secret.", "Consider the geometry. At a conventional four-way intersection, the paths of vehicles can cross or merge at thirty-two different points, and some of those crossings produce the deadliest kinds of crash: the right-angle 'T-bone' and the head-on collision during a left turn. A single-lane roundabout reduces those conflict points to eight, and none of them involves cars meeting at right angles or head-on. When collisions do happen, they tend to be glancing, low-speed sideswipes. The physics is unforgiving but simple: the energy of a moving car grows with the square of its speed, so slowing everyone down pays off disproportionately.", "The results in the real world have matched the theory. Studies of American intersections converted from stop signs or signals to roundabouts have found large drops in crashes that cause injuries, often by well over half, with even steeper declines in the most serious crashes. Roundabouts have other benefits, too. Cars spend less time idling at red lights, which saves fuel. And a roundabout keeps working during a power outage, when a traffic signal turns into a four-way guessing game.", "Critics raise fair concerns. Some drivers find roundabouts confusing. This is real, but it tends to fade quickly; surveys in communities that built roundabouts have found that public opinion, often negative beforehand, becomes much more favorable after drivers have used them for a while. Others worry about large trucks, which is why many roundabouts include a raised 'apron' around the center island that a trailer's rear wheels can ride over. Pedestrian advocates ask about people on foot, and the answer lies in design: crosswalks set back from the circle and islands that let walkers cross one direction of slow traffic at a time.", "Roundabouts are not right for every location. They need more space than a simple intersection, and some busy corridors are better served by other solutions. But too often, towns choose a traffic signal by default, because it is familiar, not because it is best. Familiarity is a poor reason to accept crashes we know how to prevent.", "The next time your town proposes a roundabout, you may hear neighbors grumble at the public meeting that it looks strange or that people will never figure it out. You might gently point out that people figured out traffic lights once, too. The circle may feel unnatural at first. So did seat belts."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses the author's central claim?", ["Traffic signals should be removed from every American town and replaced with stop signs or roundabouts right away.", "Older traffic circles are safer than modern roundabouts.", "Communities should more often choose modern roundabouts because they make intersections substantially safer.", "Drivers should be required to take a test before using a roundabout."], 2, "The author argues that towns choose signals 'by default' and that 'familiarity is a poor reason to accept crashes we know how to prevent.'"], ["What is the main purpose of the second paragraph?", ["To separate modern roundabouts from the older traffic circles readers might be imagining", "To describe the long history of traffic circles in older cities and explain why they were first built", "To argue that pinball games teach good driving habits", "To explain why roundabouts are expensive to build"], 0, "It begins 'First, a clarification' and contrasts chaotic older circles with compact modern roundabouts."], ["Which detail best explains why roundabouts reduce the most dangerous crashes?", ["Roundabouts keep working during power outages, when traffic signals stop working and drivers must guess.", "Public opinion improves after drivers use roundabouts.", "Many roundabouts have a raised apron for trucks.", "Roundabouts eliminate the right-angle and head-on conflict points found at ordinary intersections."], 3, "The author notes that of the eight conflict points, 'none of them involves cars meeting at right angles or head-on.'"], ["How does the author respond to critics of roundabouts?", ["By dismissing their concerns as foolish", "By acknowledging their concerns and explaining evidence or design features that address them", "By admitting that roundabouts are unsafe for pedestrians and should never be built near schools or busy sidewalks", "By refusing to discuss any objections"], 1, "The author calls them 'fair concerns' and answers each one: surveys, truck aprons, and pedestrian islands."], ["Why does the author end with the sentence 'So did seat belts'?", ["To suggest that seat belts are unnecessary in roundabouts", "To prove that seat belts were invented at the same time as roundabouts", "To suggest that initial discomfort with a safety measure is not a good reason to reject it", "To change the subject to car design"], 2, "The comparison implies that, like seat belts, roundabouts may feel 'unnatural at first' but save lives."]]},{"id": "r9_n6", "lv": 9, "e": "🐟", "t": "The Nose That Knows the Way Home", "p": ["Each autumn, in rivers from California to Alaska, fish that have been gone for years come home. Pacific salmon hatch in cold freshwater streams, spend their early lives there, and then travel downstream to the ocean, where they may roam thousands of miles for one to several years. Then, as adults, most of them do something that has fascinated scientists for more than a century: they return not merely to the right river, but often to the very tributary, sometimes the very stretch of gravel, where they began.", "The feat is hard to exaggerate. Imagine being driven blindfolded from your home, released in the middle of a continent, allowed to wander for three years, and then asked to find your way back to your own street without a map. Salmon manage a version of this, swimming against the current, leaping waterfalls, and navigating branching river systems in which a single wrong turn leads to the wrong stream.", "For the final stage of the journey, the evidence points to smell. In the 1950s, the biologist Arthur Hasler proposed that every stream carries a distinctive chemical signature, a blend of scents from its soil, rocks, and plants, and that young salmon memorize that signature before they leave. In a now-classic experiment, researchers captured returning salmon that had already chosen a stream, plugged the noses of some of them, and released them downstream of a fork. The fish with working noses mostly returned to their original branch. The fish with plugged noses chose seemingly at random.", "Later experiments strengthened the case. Researchers exposed young hatchery salmon to a harmless synthetic chemical, one that does not occur naturally in streams, during the period when they were preparing to migrate. Years later, when the chemical was dripped into a particular stream, many of those salmon returned to it. The fish had not been born there; they had simply learned to associate that scent with home. The process is called olfactory imprinting, and it resembles the way certain memories formed at a key moment in youth can last a lifetime.", "Smell, however, cannot explain the ocean phase of the trip. The open sea does not carry the scent of a mountain creek. There, scientists believe salmon rely on Earth's magnetic field, which varies in strength and angle from place to place. Research on sockeye salmon returning to one Canadian river found that the route they chose around a large island seemed to shift as the local magnetic field slowly changed over the years, as though the fish were following a magnetic address they had recorded as youngsters. It appears that salmon use a kind of two-part system: a magnetic map to reach the right coastline, and a nose to find the right door.", "The journey ends in a way that can seem tragic. Most Pacific salmon die soon after spawning. Yet their deaths are not wasted. Their bodies release nutrients carried from the ocean into streams that are often poor in them. Bears drag carcasses into the woods, and scientists have found ocean-derived nitrogen in the tissues of streamside trees. In some forests, part of what the trees are made of was once, in a sense, swimming in the Pacific.", "Understanding how salmon find home is not just an academic puzzle. Dams, pollution, and changes to stream chemistry can confuse or block a fish that depends on a precise memory of its birthplace. Protecting salmon means protecting not only the water, but the particular character of each stream that a young fish carries with it to sea and back."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", ["Salmon appear to use both magnetic cues and smell to return home, a journey that also benefits the ecosystems around them.", "Salmon are the only animals that can navigate using smell, which is why scientists have studied them for more than a century.", "Dams are the main reason salmon populations have declined.", "Hatchery salmon are better at finding home than wild salmon."], 0, "The passage explains the 'two-part system' of magnetism and smell and describes how salmon carcasses nourish forests."], ["Why does the author ask readers to imagine being 'driven blindfolded from your home'?", ["To explain how scientists tag salmon for research", "To warn readers about the dangers of travel", "To help readers appreciate how difficult the salmon's navigation is", "To argue that humans have a better sense of direction than fish"], 2, "The paragraph begins 'The feat is hard to exaggerate' and uses the scenario to show the difficulty of the journey."], ["Which conclusion is best supported by the nose-plugging experiment?", ["Salmon cannot swim upstream without their sense of smell.", "Salmon choose streams based on water temperature.", "Magnetic fields guide salmon within rivers.", "Smell plays an important role in how salmon choose between streams."], 3, "Fish 'with working noses mostly returned to their original branch,' while those with plugged noses 'chose seemingly at random.'"], ["Why is the experiment with the synthetic chemical especially important?", ["It proved that the synthetic chemical was harmful to salmon.", "It showed that salmon learn a home scent rather than simply being born knowing their stream.", "It showed that salmon prefer artificial scents to natural ones and will always choose them when they can.", "It explained how salmon navigate in the open ocean."], 1, "'The fish had not been born there; they had simply learned to associate that scent with home.'"], ["As used in the fifth paragraph, 'address' most nearly refers to", ["a formal speech given before a large and important audience", "a mailing label", "a specific location identified by its magnetic features", "a way of speaking to someone"], 2, "The salmon seemed to follow 'a magnetic address they had recorded as youngsters,' meaning a location marked by the field's strength and angle."]]},{"id": "r9_n7", "lv": 9, "e": "🛣️", "t": "Nana Gets Her License", "p": ["My grandmother announced that she was getting her motorcycle license at Sunday dinner, between the mashed potatoes and the pie, in the same tone she used to report that the library had extended its hours. My mother set down her fork. My uncle laughed, then stopped when he saw that no one else was laughing. Nana, who is sixty-eight and who once returned a rotisserie chicken because it was 'emotionally dry,' simply helped herself to more gravy.", "'I've already taken the safety course,' she said. 'Two days in a parking lot with nine men named Kevin. I was the only one who didn't drop the bike on the first morning.'", "'Mom,' said my mother, 'you don't even like loud noises.'", "'I don't like other people's loud noises,' said Nana. 'That's different.'", "I stayed quiet, partly because I had my own problems. I had just gotten my learner's permit, and every time I sat behind the wheel of my father's sedan, my hands sweated so much the steering wheel squeaked. My driving test was three weeks away. I had begun to suspect I would fail it, and that the examiner would remember me forever as the boy who signaled left, turned right, and apologized to a mailbox.", "So when Nana asked if I would come to the empty church parking lot on Saturday mornings and set out orange cones for her practice, I said yes. It seemed easier to watch someone else be terrified.", "Except Nana was not terrified, or if she was, she hid it somewhere I could not find. The hardest part of the test, she explained, was not speed but slowness: riding a tight figure eight inside a small box without putting a foot down. On her first attempt, she leaned too far, the motorcycle sighed, and the whole thing lay down on its side like a tired dog. Nana stepped off and looked at it. 'Well,' she said, 'that's information.'", "She picked it up the way the course had taught her, back against the seat, legs doing the work, and tried again. She dropped it twice more that morning. Each time she said something like 'Too much front brake' or 'Eyes down, that's the problem,' as though she were a detective collecting clues rather than a grandmother standing next to four hundred pounds of fallen machine. By the third Saturday, the bike stayed upright. By the fourth, she was carving the figure eight so smoothly that a man walking his beagle stopped to watch.", "I noticed something else: she never once said she was bad at it. She said the bike was heavy, the turn was tight, her eyes were in the wrong place. Every failure was a specific problem with a specific fix. I had been treating each of my own mistakes behind the wheel as proof of something permanent about myself.", "Nana passed her test on a Tuesday. I took mine eight days later. I stopped fully at the stop sign, signaled correctly, and, during the parallel parking, bumped the curb hard enough to rattle the examiner's clipboard. Instead of apologizing to the curb, I said, 'Too much wheel. That's information.' The examiner gave me a strange look, but he wrote down a passing score.", "That night Nana offered to celebrate by taking me for a ride on the back of her motorcycle. I looked at my mother's face and politely declined. Some information you do not need to gather yourself."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses a central theme of the story?", ["Older people should not take up dangerous hobbies, because their families will worry about them constantly and never relax.", "Treating failures as specific, fixable problems rather than judgments about yourself can help you improve.", "Motorcycles are easier to master than cars.", "Family members rarely support each other's goals."], 1, "The narrator notices that for Nana 'every failure was a specific problem with a specific fix,' and he adopts her approach."], ["The detail that Nana once returned a chicken for being 'emotionally dry' mainly serves to", ["humorously show that Nana is confident and a little eccentric", "prove that Nana is a skilled cook who is very picky about food", "explain why the family is so worried about her health and safety", "show that Nana is careless with money and wastes it on returns"], 0, "The odd complaint introduces Nana as someone with strong, unusual opinions, just as she calmly announces her plan."], ["Why does the narrator agree to set out cones for Nana?", ["His mother orders him to keep an eye on Nana so that she does not get hurt.", "He secretly wants to learn to ride a motorcycle himself so that he can skip driving a car.", "He needs volunteer hours for school.", "He is anxious about his own test and finds it easier to focus on someone else's challenge."], 3, "'It seemed easier to watch someone else be terrified.'"], ["When Nana says 'that's information' after dropping the bike, she shows that she", ["is embarrassed by the fall and wants to quit the lessons right away", "blames the safety course instructors for teaching her so poorly", "views the fall as useful feedback rather than a disaster", "did not even notice that the heavy bike had fallen over beside her"], 2, "She treats each drop as a clue, naming the cause ('Too much front brake') and trying again."], ["The final sentence, 'Some information you do not need to gather yourself,' mainly shows the narrator", ["admitting that he secretly failed his driving test on the first try", "applying Nana's phrase with humor to justify a cautious choice", "criticizing Nana for being reckless and setting a bad example for him", "deciding never to ride on a motorcycle for the rest of his life"], 1, "He echoes Nana's word 'information' to explain, with a joke, why he declines the ride after seeing his mother's face."]]},{"id": "r9_n8", "lv": 9, "e": "🔦", "t": "Nobody Is Looking at Your Shirt", "p": ["You walk into class and realize, too late, that there is a large stain on the front of your shirt. For the rest of the day, you are sure everyone has noticed. You keep your arms crossed. You replay each conversation, wondering whether the other person was looking at your face or at the stain. By evening, the stain has become the main event of your life. There is a good chance, however, that almost no one else saw it at all.", "Psychologists call this the spotlight effect: the tendency to overestimate how much other people notice our appearance and behavior. It is one of the most thoroughly studied quirks of the human mind, and one of the most comforting, because once you understand it, a great deal of worry starts to look like wasted energy.", "In one well-known experiment, researchers at Cornell University asked college students to put on a T-shirt printed with the face of a singer most students considered hopelessly unfashionable. Each student then walked into a room where a group of peers was already filling out questionnaires, stayed briefly, and left. Afterward, the shirt-wearers were asked to estimate how many people in the room had noticed who was on their shirt. On average, they guessed about half. In reality, only about a quarter could name the embarrassing face. The students had been sure a spotlight was trained on them. Most of the room had been looking at their own papers.", "Why do we make this mistake? Part of the answer is that each of us is the center of our own experience. You know exactly what your shirt looks like, what you said, and how nervous you feel, and that vivid knowledge is hard to set aside when you imagine how others see you. Psychologists describe this as a problem of adjustment: we start from our own perspective and fail to adjust far enough toward someone else's. The other people in the room, meanwhile, are busy starring in their own movies. To them, you are a supporting character at best, and often an extra in the background.", "A close cousin of the spotlight effect is the illusion of transparency, the belief that our inner feelings are more visible than they really are. A person giving a speech may feel as though every flutter of nerves is broadcast on a giant screen, while the audience sees someone who appears reasonably calm. Interestingly, research suggests that simply learning about this illusion can help. In one study, speakers who were told that their nervousness was less visible than they feared felt more at ease, and observers rated their speeches as better.", "None of this means that other people never notice anything. A truly unusual event, such as tripping in front of the whole cafeteria, will attract attention. But even then, people tend to judge us less harshly and forget more quickly than we expect. The embarrassment we carry for days usually lasts a few minutes in anyone else's memory, if it lasts that long.", "Understanding the spotlight effect will not make embarrassment disappear. What it can do is shrink it to its true size. The next time you are sure everyone is staring, it may help to remember that most of them are worrying about their own stains, their own awkward sentences, their own hair. In a strange way, that is freeing. If everyone is too busy with their own spotlight to watch yours, you are free to raise your hand, try the new thing, or wear the shirt."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", ["People should always dress carefully to avoid embarrassment, since others notice every stain and wrinkle.", "Public speaking is the most stressful experience most people face.", "Psychologists disagree about whether the spotlight effect exists at all, and most studies have failed to find it.", "People tend to overestimate how much others notice them, and understanding this can reduce needless worry."], 3, "The author defines the spotlight effect, supports it with research, and says understanding it can 'shrink' embarrassment 'to its true size.'"], ["What is the purpose of the stained-shirt scenario in the first paragraph?", ["To give practical advice about removing stains from clothing before school or work", "To draw readers in with a familiar experience that illustrates the idea before it is named", "To prove that most people are unkind about appearance and judge each other harshly", "To describe the procedure of the Cornell experiment in detail before revealing its results"], 1, "The relatable scene comes first, and the term 'spotlight effect' is introduced in the next paragraph."], ["According to the passage, the T-shirt experiment showed that", ["most students refused to wear the shirt", "nobody in the room noticed the shirt at all, because everyone was busy filling out questionnaires", "the shirt-wearers correctly predicted how many people noticed", "the shirt-wearers guessed about twice as many people noticed the shirt as actually did"], 3, "They 'guessed about half,' but 'only about a quarter could name the embarrassing face.'"], ["The statement that other people are 'busy starring in their own movies' suggests that", ["most people secretly want to become actors and star in real movies someday", "people tend to ignore the rules of polite behavior when they are in public", "others are mainly focused on their own concerns rather than on us", "people remember other people's embarrassing moments for many years afterward"], 2, "The author adds that to others, 'you are a supporting character at best, and often an extra in the background.'"], ["Why does the author include the study about speakers who learned about the illusion of transparency?", ["To show that knowing about this bias can have practical benefits", "To argue that people should avoid giving speeches whenever they feel nervous", "To prove that audiences are usually cruel to nervous speakers", "To suggest that nervousness is always obvious to anyone who is watching"], 0, "Speakers told about the illusion 'felt more at ease, and observers rated their speeches as better.'"]]},{"id": "r9_n9", "lv": 9, "e": "🛢️", "t": "The Great Molasses Flood", "p": ["It sounds like the setup to a joke: a wave of molasses sweeping through a city. On January 15, 1919, it was no joke at all. Shortly after noon, in Boston's crowded North End neighborhood, a giant steel storage tank split open and released more than two million gallons of thick, dark syrup into the streets. The disaster killed twenty-one people and injured about one hundred and fifty. More than a century later, it remains one of the strangest industrial accidents in American history, and one of the most instructive.", "The tank stood about fifty feet tall near the waterfront. It held molasses destined to be fermented into industrial alcohol, a product in high demand during the First World War. The company that owned it had built the tank quickly a few years earlier. Evidence gathered afterward suggested that its construction had been rushed, overseen by a manager with no engineering training, and that the tank had never been properly tested by filling it with water before it was put into service. Neighbors reported that it leaked from the start, and that brown streaks ran down its sides.", "On the day of the collapse, the tank was nearly full, and the weather had turned unusually mild after a stretch of bitter cold. Witnesses described a rumbling and a series of sharp sounds, like machine-gun fire, as the rivets holding the steel plates together gave way. Then the walls burst outward. A wave of molasses, estimated by some accounts at more than twenty feet high at its peak, rolled through the streets faster than anyone could run. It knocked buildings from their foundations, bent the steel supports of an elevated railway, and swept up wagons, horses, and people.", "Molasses may seem harmless, even comical, but its physical properties made the disaster especially deadly. It is dense and heavy, so the wave struck with tremendous force. As the January air cooled it over the following hours, the syrup grew thicker and stickier, trapping those caught in it and making rescue slow and exhausting. Sailors, soldiers, police, and neighbors waded in to help, and the cleanup took weeks. For years afterward, some residents claimed they could still smell molasses on hot summer days.", "The company at first suggested that the tank had been destroyed by a bomb, pointing to the political violence that was common in that era. Survivors and families of victims sued. The case grew into one of the largest civil lawsuits in Massachusetts history, with years of testimony from engineers, witnesses, and experts. In the end, the court-appointed investigator concluded that the tank had simply been too weak for the load it carried and that the company was responsible. It then paid substantial damages to victims and their families.", "The verdict had consequences beyond the courtroom. In the years that followed, Massachusetts and other places strengthened rules requiring that the plans for large structures be reviewed and approved by qualified engineers, with their calculations on file. Such requirements are now so routine that it is easy to forget they had to be won, often in the aftermath of tragedy.", "It is tempting to remember the Great Molasses Flood only as a piece of historical trivia, the kind of odd fact that makes people laugh in disbelief. But the real story is about something familiar: warning signs ignored, corners cut to meet demand, and the slow, necessary work of holding someone accountable. The syrup was unusual. The mistakes were not."], "q": [["Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", ["Molasses was the most important product in Boston in 1919, and the city's economy collapsed after the tank burst.", "The tank was destroyed by a bomb, as the company claimed.", "A preventable industrial failure in 1919 caused deadly damage and helped lead to stronger engineering oversight.", "Industrial alcohol was banned after the flood."], 2, "The passage describes the rushed, leaking tank, the deaths, the verdict, and the stricter rules that followed."], ["Why does the author begin by saying the event 'sounds like the setup to a joke'?", ["To acknowledge how odd the event seems while making clear that it was a serious tragedy", "To suggest that the flood was not nearly as serious as the newspapers of the time reported", "To make fun of the people of Boston", "To show that the author doubts the historical records"], 0, "The next sentence says, 'it was no joke at all,' and gives the death toll."], ["Which detail best supports the idea that the disaster could have been prevented?", ["The weather had turned unusually mild after a stretch of bitter cold that January.", "The molasses was going to be made into industrial alcohol for use during the First World War.", "Sailors and soldiers helped with the rescue.", "The tank leaked from the start and was never properly tested by filling it with water."], 3, "These warning signs show the tank's weakness was knowable before it failed."], ["According to the fourth paragraph, why did the cold make things worse after the collapse?", ["It froze the harbor, blocking rescue boats.", "The cooling syrup thickened, trapping people and slowing rescue.", "It caused a second tank to burst.", "It made the streets too icy for rescuers to walk on."], 1, "'As the January air cooled it... the syrup grew thicker and stickier, trapping those caught in it.'"], ["What does the author suggest with the final lines, 'The syrup was unusual. The mistakes were not'?", ["Molasses is still stored in dangerous tanks in Boston.", "The disaster could only have happened in 1919.", "The lessons of the disaster still apply to many situations today.", "The company made no real mistakes."], 2, "The author says the real story is about 'something familiar: warning signs ignored, corners cut.'"]]},{"id": "r9_n10", "lv": 9, "e": "🎃", "t": "The Weigh-Off", "p": ["My grandfather named his pumpkins the way other people name ships. There had been Vesuvius, which rotted in August, and The Senator, which split during a heat wave, and now there was Big Rosa, who sat in a hollow of straw behind his garage like a sunburned planet. Every morning that summer he measured her with a cloth tape, consulted a chart, and announced her estimated weight to me with the gravity of a news anchor. By September she was, according to the chart, nine hundred and twelve pounds.", "'She hears music,' he told me, and he meant it. He played opera for her from a paint-spattered radio, because he believed the vibrations were good for the vine. When I pointed out that there was no evidence for this, he said there was no evidence against it either, and that at his age he was not willing to take chances with Puccini.", "The only thing he loved more than Big Rosa was his feud with Mr. Pettibone. Mr. Pettibone lived three houses down and also grew giant pumpkins, and for eleven years the two men had competed at the county weigh-off with the intensity of generals. They did not speak. They communicated through the local newspaper's garden column, where each was occasionally quoted saying something mild and deadly about 'certain growers' who 'overwatered.'", "On the morning of the weigh-off, it took four neighbors, a borrowed trailer, and a lifting harness to move Big Rosa. At the fairgrounds, the inspectors walked around her slowly with flashlights. I watched my grandfather's face while they did it. Then one of them knelt near the base, where the pumpkin had rested on the straw, and ran his finger along a line I had never noticed. He shook his head. The crack went all the way into the hollow center. By the rules, Big Rosa could be displayed, but she could not be weighed for a prize.", "My grandfather took it quietly, which was worse than if he had shouted. He patted her side twice, as if calming a horse, and went to stand by the fence.", "An hour later, Mr. Pettibone's pumpkin went onto the scale: eight hundred and seventy pounds, enough to win. I expected my grandfather to leave. Instead, when the forklift operator struggled to lower the winner back onto its pallet and the pumpkin began to tip, it was my grandfather who ran forward, braced it with both hands, and shouted instructions until it settled safely. Mr. Pettibone stood beside him, breathing hard. For a moment, the two old men were holding the same pumpkin.", "'You'd have beaten me,' Mr. Pettibone said.", "'By forty-two pounds,' said my grandfather, who had done the math before the forklift stopped.", "Driving home, I asked him why he had helped. He was quiet for a mile. Then he told me that twelve years ago, when Mr. Pettibone's wife was sick, my grandfather had given him a packet of seeds to take his mind off things, and that the whole rivalry had grown from that single packet, like a vine that got out of hand. 'Somewhere along the way,' he said, 'we forgot we started out on the same side.'", "That winter, my grandfather saved the seeds from Big Rosa, who, cracked or not, had been the biggest pumpkin he ever grew. In March, I watched him walk three houses down with an envelope in his hand. The garden column that spring reported that 'certain growers' had begun sharing a seed line. It also reported that one of them had started playing opera to his vines, and that the other considered this 'unscientific but not unreasonable.'"], "q": [["Which statement best expresses a central theme of the story?", ["Rivalries can hide a shared history, and a moment of generosity can help restore it.", "Winning a competition is the best way to earn respect from neighbors and from the whole town.", "Giant pumpkins are too fragile to be worth growing, since they crack so easily before a weigh-off.", "Older people should not compete against their neighbors because it always ruins friendships."], 0, "The grandfather helps his rival, recalls that 'we started out on the same side,' and shares his seeds."], ["The grandfather's argument that there was 'no evidence against' playing opera to his pumpkin mainly reveals his", ["deep and scientific knowledge of plant biology", "wish to annoy his neighbors with loud music", "stubborn, humorous devotion to his pumpkin", "strong dislike of all modern and popular music"], 2, "He meant it about the music, and he is 'not willing to take chances with Puccini,' which shows playful but serious devotion."], ["The detail about the garden column in the third paragraph mainly shows that the rivalry", ["was friendly and completely good-natured from the very beginning", "was reported as front-page news in every issue of the paper", "involved physical fights between the two men at the fairgrounds", "was carried out through indirect, polite-sounding insults"], 3, "Each man was quoted 'saying something mild and deadly about certain growers who overwatered.'"], ["The narrator says his grandfather's quiet reaction 'was worse than if he had shouted' because", ["the narrator was embarrassed by the large crowd at the fair", "the quietness showed how deeply disappointed he was", "the grandfather was quietly planning revenge on Mr. Pettibone", "the inspectors had made a mistake that no one would admit"], 1, "His silence and the way he 'patted her side twice... and went to stand by the fence' reveal hidden disappointment."], ["How does the final paragraph connect to earlier parts of the story?", ["It reveals that Big Rosa was secretly weighed after the fair was over and would have won the prize.", "It shows that Mr. Pettibone moved away.", "It echoes the garden column and the opera to show the rivalry becoming a friendly exchange.", "It explains that the narrator will take over the pumpkin patch."], 2, "The 'certain growers' phrase and the opera return, now describing seed-sharing and gentle teasing."]]},{"id": "r10_n1", "lv": 10, "e": "📰", "t": "The Fishing Report", "p": ["For thirty-one years, the fishing report in the Cedar Fork Gazette had been written by Harlan Voss, and for thirty-one years no one had been entirely sure how much of it was true. The report appeared every Thursday on page six, between the obituaries and the classified ads, and it was the first thing most of the town read. It told readers where the crappie were schooling and what the bass were biting. It also told them, on one memorable occasion, about a smallmouth of such dignity that it had declined to be photographed, and on another about a catfish in Miller's Slough that was 'known to the older residents and feared by the younger ones.'", "Tess Albright became editor of the Gazette in March. She was twenty-six, had worked two years at a city paper that no longer existed, and had brought with her a laminated card listing the principles of responsible journalism, which she taped above her desk. Principle number three was 'Verify before you publish.' In her second week, she read Harlan's column, reread it, and called him into the office.", "He arrived in waders, which she suspected was a strategic choice. She explained, as gently as she could, that the paper would now need a source for any specific claim. The eleven-pound walleye, for instance. Who had caught it? Was there a photograph, a scale receipt, a witness?", "Harlan considered this. 'The walleye,' he said, 'is a composite.'", "The following Thursday, the fishing report was accurate. It listed the water temperature at the dam, the level of the reservoir, and the fact that two anglers had reported catching bluegill near the boat ramp, neither of whom wished to be named. It was eighty-seven words long. It contained no dignity of any kind, human or fish.", "The response was immediate. Three people canceled their subscriptions. The owner of the bait shop, a woman who had never before expressed an opinion about journalism, called to say that business had dropped, because people came in on Thursdays to argue about the column. Someone slid an anonymous note under the office door that read, in its entirety, 'Where is the catfish.'", "Tess did what she had been trained to do: she went to the source. At five-thirty on a gray Saturday, she climbed into the front of Harlan's aluminum boat with a notebook and a thermos. For four hours they caught nothing, yet she was not bored for a moment. Harlan narrated the lake as they crossed it, the way someone else might narrate a city he had lived in all his life. The weed line had shifted since spring. Runoff from the new subdivision had clouded the east cove, and the fish had moved out of it. A heron worked the shallows every morning at the same stump, and where the heron fished, he said, the minnows were, and where the minnows were, sooner or later, something larger would be. He was, she realized, never wrong about the water. He was only generous about the fish.", "Rowing back, he admitted that the eleven-pound walleye was a combination of three walleyes, caught by three different people over two summers, and that he had combined them because none of the individual fish had quite captured the spirit of the season. She wrote this down. She could not tell whether it was the most dishonest thing she had ever heard or a fairly accurate description of editing.", "The next Thursday, the fishing report returned to its former length. Above it, in small type, Tess added a line: 'Conditions verified. Fish reported in good faith.' The water temperatures were real. So was the note about the east cove, which led, two weeks later, to a front-page story about the subdivision's drainage, the first story the Gazette had broken in years. Below the conditions, Harlan reported that the catfish in Miller's Slough had been seen again, 'looking, if anything, larger, and in no mood to be verified.'", "The laminated card stayed above Tess's desk. She did not take it down. But beneath principle number three, she added a note in pen: 'Know what kind of truth you're looking for.'"], "q": [["Which choice best describes a central development in the passage?", ["A veteran columnist is fired after the new editor discovers that he has been exaggerating his reports for decades.", "An editor committed to strict verification comes to appreciate the different kinds of truth in a colorful column.", "A small-town newspaper goes out of business after losing subscribers.", "A young journalist learns to fish and abandons her career."], 1, "Tess moves from demanding a source for every fish to writing 'Know what kind of truth you're looking for.'"], ["Harlan's statement that the walleye 'is a composite' primarily serves to", ["show that he genuinely misunderstands the question that Tess is asking him", "suggest that he invented the fish entirely from nothing, with no real catch behind it", "prove that the fish was photographed from several different angles by witnesses", "reveal, with deadpan humor, that his account blended several real catches"], 3, "He later admits the walleye 'was a combination of three walleyes, caught by three different people.'"], ["The reactions described in the sixth paragraph mainly suggest that", ["the column's value to the community went beyond the accuracy of its fish reports", "readers prefer false information to true information, as long as it is entertaining to read", "the bait shop owner disliked Harlan", "the town had lost interest in fishing"], 0, "People canceled subscriptions, the bait shop lost its Thursday arguments, and a reader demanded 'Where is the catfish.'"], ["As used in the seventh paragraph, 'generous' most nearly means", ["wealthy and fond of spending money freely", "forgiving of other people's faults", "inclined to enlarge or embellish", "willing to share food and supplies"], 2, "Harlan is 'never wrong about the water' but 'only generous about the fish,' meaning he makes the fish bigger and grander."], ["The front-page drainage story in the ninth paragraph primarily serves to", ["suggest that Tess wanted to replace Harlan with a more careful and reliable writer from her old city paper", "show that Harlan's close observations of the lake contained reliable, valuable information", "reveal that the catfish was real", "criticize the town for building new houses"], 1, "His note about the clouded east cove was accurate and 'led... to a front-page story.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n2", "lv": 10, "e": "🧊", "t": "Does Hot Water Freeze Faster?", "p": ["Ask a room full of people whether hot water freezes faster than cold, and you will likely start a spirited argument. Some will insist the idea is obviously false: hot water has farther to go, so it must take longer. Others will swear they have seen it happen, in ice cube trays or on winter mornings. The claim has an unusually long history. Aristotle mentioned a version of it more than two thousand years ago, and later thinkers, including Francis Bacon and René Descartes, discussed it as well. Yet in the modern era it is best known by the name of a schoolboy.", "In the 1960s, Erasto Mpemba, a secondary-school student in Tanzania, noticed during a cooking class that a hot mixture for ice cream seemed to freeze before a cooler one. When he asked his teachers about it, he was told that he must be confused. He persisted, and when a visiting physicist, Denis Osborne, gave a lecture at his school, Mpemba asked the question again. Osborne, to his credit, went back to his laboratory and tried it. The two published a paper together in 1969, and the phenomenon has been called the Mpemba effect ever since.", "At first glance, the effect seems to violate common sense, and perhaps physics. But a surprising number of plausible mechanisms have been proposed. Hot water evaporates faster, so a hot sample may lose some of its mass before freezing, leaving less water to cool. Heating drives out dissolved gases, which might change how water freezes. Temperature differences within a hot container create stronger convection currents, which could speed the loss of heat from the surface. A warm container might melt the frost beneath it in a freezer and make better contact with the cold shelf. Some researchers have pointed to supercooling, the tendency of water to remain liquid below its normal freezing point.", "The abundance of explanations should have been a warning sign. When a phenomenon has a dozen possible causes, it is worth asking whether it reliably exists at all. That is exactly what several later researchers did. The difficulty begins with a deceptively simple question: what counts as 'freezing'? Is it the moment the first crystals of ice appear, the moment the water reaches zero degrees Celsius, or the moment the sample is solid throughout? Each definition can produce a different answer. So can small details such as the shape of the container, the type of freezer, and, critically, where exactly the thermometer is placed.", "In 2016, a pair of researchers in the United Kingdom set out to test the effect under carefully controlled conditions, measuring how long water at different starting temperatures took to cool to zero degrees. They found no evidence that hot water got there first. They also noted that moving a thermometer by as little as a centimeter could change the measured result enough to create the appearance of an effect. Reviewing the published literature, they concluded that few earlier studies had been designed in a way that could establish the effect convincingly.", "That finding did not settle every argument. Supercooling, in particular, is notoriously unpredictable, and it remains possible that under certain specific conditions a warmer sample might form ice first. Physicists have also identified related effects in other, carefully engineered systems, in which a system that starts farther from equilibrium can, under the right circumstances, settle into it faster. What has become clear is that the popular version of the claim, the idea that hot water in your freezer will simply and reliably freeze faster than cold, stands on much weaker ground than its reputation suggests.", "The story of the Mpemba effect is valuable less for its answer than for its lessons. The first is that a student's question deserves a real hearing; Mpemba's teachers were wrong to dismiss him without testing his claim. The second, which is easier to forget, is that a real hearing includes the possibility that the student's observation, however sincere, reflected particular conditions rather than a general law. Curiosity and skepticism are often presented as opposites. In practice, the best science requires both, sometimes applied to the same question on the same afternoon."], "q": [["Which choice best states the central idea of the passage?", ["Hot water always freezes faster than cold water because it evaporates much more quickly and loses mass before it cools.", "Students are usually more reliable observers than their teachers.", "The claim that hot water freezes faster has a long history, and careful testing has cast doubt on its popular version.", "Thermometers are too inaccurate to be used in scientific research."], 2, "The passage traces the claim from Aristotle to Mpemba and reports that controlled tests 'found no evidence that hot water got there first.'"], ["The author mentions Aristotle, Bacon, and Descartes primarily to", ["establish that the claim has puzzled thinkers for a very long time", "prove that ancient scientists were correct about freezing all along", "contrast European science with the science taught in African schools", "suggest that famous thinkers are rarely wrong about everyday things"], 0, "The author notes that 'the claim has an unusually long history' before introducing these names."], ["The author suggests that 'the abundance of explanations should have been a warning sign' because", ["scientists should never propose more than one possible hypothesis to explain any single observation they make", "each explanation was proven false in 1969", "convection currents do not occur in freezers", "many competing explanations can indicate that the phenomenon itself has not been firmly established"], 3, "'When a phenomenon has a dozen possible causes, it is worth asking whether it reliably exists at all.'"], ["As used in the fourth paragraph, 'deceptively' most nearly means", ["dishonestly", "misleadingly", "cleverly", "completely"], 1, "The question of what counts as freezing seems simple but turns out to be complicated, so its simplicity is misleading."], ["In the final paragraph, the author's point about curiosity and skepticism is best described as", ["a complaint that scientists are far too skeptical of the observations made by young people", "a claim that curiosity is more important than skepticism in every scientific question", "an argument that good inquiry combines open-minded questioning with rigorous testing", "a warning that students should not question their teachers, even when they notice something odd"], 2, "'Curiosity and skepticism are often presented as opposites. In practice, the best science requires both.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n3", "lv": 10, "e": "🐀", "t": "The Rat Tail Problem", "p": ["In 1902, the French colonial administration in Hanoi faced a rat problem. The city had recently built a modern sewer system, and the rats had moved in, thriving in the dark tunnels beneath the handsome new European quarter. With the threat of plague looming, officials hired rat catchers and, when that proved too slow, turned to the public, offering a small bounty for each rat killed. To spare themselves the unpleasant task of counting carcasses, they asked only for proof: a rat's tail.", "At first, the policy appeared to be a success. Tails poured in by the thousands. But officials soon began noticing rats running through the streets without tails. Enterprising residents had realized that a live rat was worth more than a dead one. Cut off the tail, collect the bounty, and release the animal to breed, producing more rats and, therefore, more tails. There were even reports of people raising rats for profit. The program, designed to reduce the rat population, had given people a reason to maintain it.", "Economists call this kind of outcome a perverse incentive: a reward that, by its structure, encourages the very behavior it was meant to prevent. A similar story is often told about colonial Delhi, where a bounty on cobras supposedly led people to breed cobras, and where the eventual cancellation of the bounty led breeders to release their now-worthless snakes, leaving the city with more cobras than before. That story is so tidy that it has given its name to the whole phenomenon, the 'cobra effect.' Historians, though, have found little documentary evidence for it. The Hanoi rat hunt, by contrast, is well recorded, which makes it the better lesson, and a reminder that a good story is not the same as a good source.", "The deeper problem in both tales is not greed; people responding to rewards is exactly what the officials were counting on. The problem is the gap between what the officials wanted and what they measured. They wanted fewer rats. They paid for tails. As long as tails and dead rats traveled together, the measure worked. The moment it became possible to separate them, the measure drifted away from the goal, and people followed the measure.", "This pattern is common enough to be summarized in a single principle: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. Economists associate the idea with Goodhart's law, named for a British economist who described a version of it in the context of monetary policy. Its reach, however, extends far beyond economics. A school judged only by test scores may drift toward teaching test-taking. A call center that rewards employees for short calls may find its representatives ending calls before problems are solved. An old joke describes a factory told to produce nails by weight that responds by making a few enormous, useless nails, and then, when the target is changed to quantity, producing millions of tiny ones. The joke endures because it is only a slight exaggeration of real experience.", "None of this means that measurement is useless. Without some way of counting, the officials in Hanoi could not have known whether their efforts were working at all. The lesson is subtler. Numbers are indispensable for understanding a situation, but they become fragile the moment people's rewards depend on them. A thermometer is useful precisely because no one is paying it to report a particular temperature.", "Those who design incentives have learned a few defenses. One is to measure outcomes as close to the real goal as possible, such as the number of plague cases rather than the number of tails. Another is to use several measures at once, so that gaming one produces an obvious mismatch with the others. A third is to imagine, before a program begins, how a clever and self-interested person might exploit it, and to ask that question with genuine imagination. The officials in Hanoi were not foolish. They simply failed to picture a resident looking at a rat and seeing not a pest but a small, renewable source of income.", "In the end, the rat tail problem is less about rats than about the limits of control. Any system that rewards people will be studied by the people it rewards, often more carefully than by the people who designed it. The best planners assume as much from the start."], "q": [["Which choice best states the central idea of the passage?", ["Colonial governments were uniquely bad at public health and ignored the advice of nearly every doctor and scientist they consulted.", "Paying people for results never works.", "Rats were the main cause of plague in the twentieth century.", "Rewards tied to a measure can backfire when the measure can be separated from the goal it was meant to track."], 3, "'They wanted fewer rats. They paid for tails... The moment it became possible to separate them, the measure drifted away from the goal.'"], ["The author's discussion of the 'cobra effect' in the third paragraph primarily serves to", ["prove that the Delhi cobra story is entirely accurate and well documented by historians", "contrast a famous but poorly documented example with a well-documented one", "argue that snakes are far more dangerous to city residents than rats are", "explain how the term 'perverse incentive' was first invented by economists"], 1, "Historians 'have found little documentary evidence' for the cobra story, while 'the Hanoi rat hunt, by contrast, is well recorded.'"], ["The thermometer comparison in the sixth paragraph mainly illustrates that", ["a measure stays reliable when no one is rewarded for producing a particular result", "thermometers are the single most accurate scientific instruments ever invented by humans", "temperature is more important than other data", "officials in Hanoi relied on weather data"], 0, "'A thermometer is useful precisely because no one is paying it to report a particular temperature.'"], ["As used in the seventh paragraph, 'genuine' most nearly means", ["expensive", "official", "authentic", "inherited"], 2, "Planners should imagine exploitation with real, authentic imagination rather than a token effort."], ["Based on the passage, which policy would the author most likely consider best designed?", ["A reward paid for each rat tail turned in, with no other checks on how the tails were obtained", "A public health program that rewards reductions in actual disease cases, checked against other data", "A bounty on cobras that is canceled suddenly and without any warning to the people who have been breeding them", "A school rating system based only on a single test score"], 1, "The author recommends measuring 'outcomes as close to the real goal as possible' and using 'several measures at once.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n4", "lv": 10, "e": "🚐", "t": "The Carpool", "p": ["Denise had learned, over four years of driving the morning carpool, that a minivan is a kind of confessional in which the priest is assumed to be deaf. Somewhere around the second week of any school year, the boys in the back forgot she was there. She became part of the vehicle, like the cup holders or the persistent smell of a granola bar lost beneath a seat years ago and never found. She did not mind. She had discovered that she learned more about her son's life in twelve minutes on Route 9 than in a month of dinner-table questions.", "She learned who liked whom, which teachers were 'fine' and which were 'actually insane,' and that a particular video game had undergone an update so catastrophic that Owen and his friends discussed it with the gravity of a funeral. She learned to keep her eyes on the road and her face arranged in an expression of pleasant emptiness, the face of a woman thinking about groceries. Any reaction, she knew, would break the spell. A single raised eyebrow in the mirror, and the back seat would fall silent for a week.", "On a Tuesday in October, the conversation turned to a boy named Felix. Felix had worn something to school the day before, some combination of a cape and a backpack, or possibly a cape that was also a backpack; Denise could not follow the details. What she could follow was the tone. The laughter in the back seat had an edge she recognized from her own school years, a sharpness that was less about humor than about establishing who stood where. Tyler did an impression. Marcus added a detail. And then Owen laughed too, not loudly, but enough.", "Denise's hands tightened on the wheel. She knew Felix. More precisely, she knew that Felix and Owen had spent three summers at the same day camp, that they had once built an elaborate fort from cardboard boxes in her garage, and that Felix had cried when it went out with the recycling. She glanced in the mirror. Owen was looking out the window, and something in the angle of his shoulders suggested that he was not entirely comfortable with the sound of his own laugh.", "The speech she wanted to give assembled itself instantly, complete with a thesis and supporting examples. She could feel it pressing against her teeth. She also knew precisely what would happen if she gave it. The boys would go quiet. Owen would be mortified in front of his friends, which would make him defend himself, which would turn a careless moment into a position he had to hold. And she would lose the carpool, not the driving but the listening, and with it one of the last open windows into her son's life.", "So she said nothing, and felt terrible about it for the rest of the morning.", "That afternoon, she did something she had not planned. At pickup, she saw Felix standing alone near the flagpole, a regular backpack over one shoulder and no cape anywhere in sight. She rolled down the window and asked if he needed a ride. He hesitated, then climbed into the far back row, where Owen, already buckled in, looked up at him with an expression Denise could not read.", "For the first few minutes, no one spoke. Denise turned on the radio and kept her face in its grocery arrangement. Then, somewhere past the hardware store, she heard Felix say, 'Remember the fort?' And after a pause long enough to make her heart pound, Owen said, 'The one with the tunnel? Your mom made us take it down because of the raccoon.'", "'It was one raccoon,' said Felix.", "'It was a big raccoon,' said Owen, and they both laughed, and it was a different laugh from the morning's, rounder, with nothing sharp in it.", "Denise did not look in the mirror. She did not want to see anything that might tempt her to speak. She was not naive enough to think that a single ride would undo the morning, or that Owen would not laugh at the wrong thing again tomorrow, with Tyler and Marcus beside him. She knew that the pull of a group is stronger than the memory of a cardboard fort. But she had placed two boys in the same row, with a shared story between them, and left the rest to them. It was, she thought, the only kind of speech a mother could give that her son might actually hear."], "q": [["Which choice best describes a central development in the passage?", ["A mother who overhears her son join in mocking a classmate responds indirectly by giving that classmate a ride.", "A mother lectures her son's friends about kindness during the drive and, as a result, loses her place in the carpool.", "Two boys build a new fort together after years apart.", "A boy confesses to his mother that he feels pressured by his friends to tease others at school."], 0, "Denise stays silent in the morning, then offers Felix a ride so the two boys share the back row."], ["The comparison of the minivan to 'a confessional in which the priest is assumed to be deaf' primarily suggests that", ["Denise disapproves of nearly everything the boys say in the back seat", "the minivan is far too noisy for any real conversation to happen", "the boys speak openly because they forget Denise is listening", "Denise is hard of hearing and misses most of what the boys say"], 2, "'Somewhere around the second week of any school year, the boys in the back forgot she was there.'"], ["Based on the fifth paragraph, Denise decides not to speak mainly because she believes that", ["the boys' joke was harmless and not worth mentioning", "a lecture would embarrass Owen, harden his behavior, and end her chance to listen", "Felix deserved to be teased for wearing a cape to school", "Owen would not care at all what his mother thought, since he no longer listens to her advice"], 1, "She foresees that Owen would 'defend himself' and that she would 'lose... the listening.'"], ["As used in the final paragraph, 'pull' most nearly means", ["a physical tug", "a handle on a drawer", "an unfair advantage", "a strong influence"], 3, "'The pull of a group' refers to the power a group of friends has over a person's behavior."], ["The final sentence of the passage mainly suggests that Denise views her action as", ["a mistake that she will regret for a long time afterward", "a way to punish Owen quietly for his behavior that morning", "a message delivered through action rather than words", "a lecture she plans to give Owen later that night at dinner"], 2, "She calls the ride 'the only kind of speech a mother could give that her son might actually hear,' contrasting it with the spoken speech she held back."]]},{"id": "r10_n5", "lv": 10, "e": "🌉", "t": "The Bridge That Galloped", "p": ["On the morning of November 7, 1940, a steady wind of around forty miles an hour blew through the Tacoma Narrows, a strait of Puget Sound in Washington State. It was a strong wind, but not an extraordinary one. By late morning, it had torn apart what was then the third-longest suspension bridge span in the world, open to traffic for barely four months. The collapse was captured on film, and the footage of the bridge's deck twisting like a ribbon before plunging into the water remains among the most famous images in the history of engineering.", "The bridge had been controversial even before it fell. Its designers had pursued a look that was fashionable among engineers of the time: long, slender, and graceful. Many earlier suspension bridges stiffened their roadways with deep, open steel trusses, lattices that allowed wind to pass through. The Tacoma Narrows Bridge instead used shallow, solid girders along the edges of a deck that carried only two lanes of traffic. The result was elegant and comparatively inexpensive. It was also, as would soon become clear, unusually flexible.", "Almost from opening day, the deck rose and fell in waves when the wind blew, sometimes by several feet. Drivers reported watching the cars ahead of them disappear into a trough and reappear on the next crest. Locals nicknamed the bridge Galloping Gertie, and some people drove across it simply for the thrill. Engineers studied the motion and considered remedies, but the bridge's movement, though alarming, was not yet thought to be fatal.", "On November 7, the character of the motion changed. The familiar up-and-down waves gave way to a violent twisting, with one side of the deck rising as the other fell, the roadway tilting steeply first one way and then the other. Traffic had been halted. The last vehicle on the span was abandoned by its driver, who crawled to safety; a dog left inside the car was lost when the bridge fell. No person died. Around eleven o'clock, a large section of the center span broke free and dropped into the water below.", "For decades, textbooks presented the collapse as a classic example of resonance, comparing it to a singer shattering a wine glass by matching its natural frequency. According to this account, the wind must have pushed on the bridge with a rhythm that matched the bridge's own. The explanation is appealing, but engineers who have studied the event closely consider it misleading. A steady wind has no single rhythm to match. The more accurate explanation is a phenomenon called aeroelastic flutter, in which the motion of a structure changes the way the wind acts on it, and the altered wind force, in turn, amplifies the motion. Once the deck began to twist, each twist caught the wind in a way that fed energy into the next. The bridge, in effect, was helping the wind destroy it.", "The distinction matters. Resonance suggests an unlucky coincidence of timing, the sort of problem that might be avoided by adjusting a number. Flutter points to something more fundamental: a relationship between shape and airflow that the designers had not understood and that the engineering methods of the time could not predict. The bridge's builders had calculated its response to wind largely as though wind simply pressed against a surface. They had not accounted for the possibility that the surface itself could reshape the forces acting on it.", "The disaster transformed bridge engineering. Afterward, scale models of major bridges came to be routinely tested in wind tunnels, and the study of how structures interact with moving air grew into a field of its own. When a replacement bridge opened at the same site in 1950, it had a deep, open stiffening truss and far greater resistance to twisting. Engineers today still design long bridges with the lessons of that November morning in mind.", "It is easy to watch the film and see only failure. But the collapse also illustrates how engineering advances: not by avoiding every mistake, which is impossible, but by studying mistakes with honesty. The Tacoma Narrows Bridge was the product of confident assumptions that turned out to be incomplete. What followed it was the product of people willing to ask exactly how, and why, they had been wrong."], "q": [["Which choice best states the central idea of the passage?", ["The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapsed because it was struck by an unusually violent storm with winds far stronger than anything engineers had expected.", "The collapse resulted from a poorly understood interaction between wind and structure and led to lasting changes in engineering.", "Suspension bridges are too dangerous to build over water.", "Resonance is the most common cause of bridge failures."], 1, "The passage explains aeroelastic flutter and says 'the disaster transformed bridge engineering.'"], ["The second paragraph serves mainly to", ["describe the backgrounds of the people who designed and built the bridge", "argue that elegant designs are always unsafe and should be avoided", "compare the costs of different bridges built during the same years", "explain design choices that made the bridge unusually flexible"], 3, "Shallow solid girders and a narrow deck made the bridge 'elegant and comparatively inexpensive' but 'unusually flexible.'"], ["According to the passage, engineers consider the resonance explanation misleading because", ["a steady wind lacks a single rhythm that could match the bridge's natural frequency", "the bridge had no natural frequency at all, because its long deck was far too flexible to vibrate", "singers cannot really shatter wine glasses", "the wind that day was far too weak to move a bridge of that size"], 0, "'A steady wind has no single rhythm to match.'"], ["As used in the sixth paragraph, 'fundamental' most nearly means", ["introductory", "underlying", "financial", "permanent"], 1, "Flutter points to a deeper, underlying problem in the relationship 'between shape and airflow,' not a simple timing coincidence."], ["The author's attitude in the final paragraph is best described as", ["bitter criticism of the bridge's designers and their careless choices", "amused indifference toward the disaster and the lessons it offered", "reflective appreciation of how failure can drive progress", "anxious warning that similar collapses are likely to happen again soon"], 2, "Engineering advances 'by studying mistakes with honesty,' and the author praises those 'willing to ask exactly how, and why, they had been wrong.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n6", "lv": 10, "e": "🥱", "t": "The Uses of Boredom", "p": ["In one of the more unsettling experiments in recent psychology, researchers asked volunteers to sit alone in a plain room for about fifteen minutes with nothing to do but think. They had no phones, no books, no paper. Earlier in the session, the volunteers had felt a mild electric shock, and many had said they would pay money to avoid feeling it again. Yet the room contained a button that would deliver that same shock, and a striking number of participants pressed it. About two-thirds of the men and a quarter of the women chose to shock themselves at least once rather than simply sit with their thoughts. One participant pressed the button about 190 times, an outlier the researchers noted with understandable puzzlement.", "The study is often cited as evidence of how much people dislike boredom, and it certainly shows that. But it also raises a question our culture seldom asks: what, if anything, is boredom for? We treat it as a malfunction, a gap in stimulation to be filled as quickly as possible. The modern world is extraordinarily good at filling it. A person waiting in a checkout line in 1990 had little choice but to stand there. The same person today can scroll through news, messages, and videos, eliminating boredom before it has quite arrived.", "Researchers who study boredom increasingly argue that the emotion is not a flaw but a signal. Like hunger, which tells us to find food, boredom tells us that our current activity is failing to engage us and that it may be time to look for something more meaningful. In this view, boredom is uncomfortable for the same reason pain is uncomfortable: its job is to get our attention. A signal that never hurt would be a signal no one obeyed.", "There is also evidence that boredom can nudge the mind toward creativity. In one study, participants who first spent time on a deliberately tedious task, copying numbers from a phone directory, later came up with more ideas when asked to think of uses for a pair of plastic cups than participants who had skipped the tedious task. The effect was modest, and a single study should not be treated as a law. But it fits a broader observation: when the mind is not occupied by outside input, it tends to wander, and wandering minds rehearse memories, imagine future plans, and connect ideas that focused attention keeps apart. Many people report that their best thoughts arrive in the shower or on a long walk, precisely the moments when nothing else demands their attention.", "If boredom is a signal, then the habit of silencing it instantly may carry a cost. A person who reaches for a phone the moment a lull appears is, in effect, turning off the alarm without ever checking what it was about. The discomfort disappears, but so does the prompt to ask whether one's time is being spent well. Over months and years, this may leave people entertained but oddly adrift, never quite bored and never quite engaged.", "It is important not to romanticize the feeling. Chronic, inescapable boredom, the kind experienced in monotonous work or genuine isolation, is associated with real harm, and no one should be told that their suffering is secretly good for them. Nor is every idle moment a doorway to insight; sometimes a bored mind simply produces more boredom. The argument here is narrower. It is that brief, ordinary boredom, the ten minutes in a waiting room, the slow afternoon, the car ride without a screen, is not an emergency, and that treating it as one may deprive us of something useful.", "What might tolerating boredom look like in practice? It might mean leaving the phone in a pocket during a short wait and noticing what the mind does instead. It might mean allowing children a stretch of unscheduled time without rushing to fill it, trusting that the complaint 'there's nothing to do' often comes just before the invention of something to do. It might mean taking a walk without headphones.", "None of this requires heroic discipline. It requires only the willingness to sit, for a little while, in the room without the button, and to discover that the room is not as empty as it first appears."], "q": [["The author's central claim is best summarized as which of the following?", ["Boredom is a serious illness that should be treated by doctors before it causes lasting harm to the mind.", "Brief, ordinary boredom serves a useful purpose, so people should be more willing to tolerate it.", "Smartphones should be banned from public places.", "Men handle boredom worse than women do."], 1, "The author argues that 'brief, ordinary boredom... is not an emergency, and that treating it as one may deprive us of something useful.'"], ["The author describes the electric shock experiment at the beginning primarily to", ["give a vivid illustration of how strongly people dislike being alone with their thoughts", "argue that psychological experiments are often cruel to the volunteers who take part in them", "prove that boredom causes physical pain", "show that most people enjoy mild electric shocks"], 0, "Many participants chose a shock they had said they'd pay to avoid 'rather than simply sit with their thoughts.'"], ["In the fourth paragraph, the statement that 'a single study should not be treated as a law' serves to", ["reject the study's findings entirely as unreliable and useless", "show that the author deeply distrusts all psychology research", "introduce a second, larger study that found the opposite result", "qualify the evidence while still presenting it as suggestive"], 3, "The author admits the effect 'was modest' but says it 'fits a broader observation' about wandering minds."], ["As used in the sixth paragraph, 'romanticize' most nearly means", ["fall in love with", "write poetry about", "idealize", "criticize"], 2, "The author warns against making boredom seem better than it is, noting that chronic boredom 'is associated with real harm.'"], ["The final paragraph's reference to 'the room without the button' mainly serves to", ["suggest that the original experiment should be repeated with more volunteers and longer sessions", "criticize the researchers for including a button", "warn readers about the dangers of electricity", "return to the opening experiment to recast boredom as an opportunity rather than a threat"], 3, "It echoes the opening study and suggests 'the room is not as empty as it first appears.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n7", "lv": 10, "e": "🍄", "t": "The Wood Wide Web, Reconsidered", "p": ["Few scientific ideas of recent decades have captured the public imagination quite like the 'wood wide web.' In its most popular version, the trees of a forest are linked underground by a vast network of fungal threads, through which they share food, send warnings about insect attacks, and care for their young. Older trees, sometimes called 'mother trees,' are said to recognize their own seedlings and nourish them through the network. The idea has inspired bestselling books, documentaries, and at least one celebrated novel. It has changed the way many people look at a forest. The question scientists have increasingly been asking is how much of it is true.", "The foundation of the idea is solid. Most land plants form partnerships with mycorrhizal fungi, organisms whose threadlike filaments, called hyphae, wrap around or penetrate plant roots and extend far into the surrounding soil. The fungi help the plant absorb water and nutrients such as phosphorus and nitrogen, and in return the plant supplies the fungi with sugars produced through photosynthesis. This exchange is ancient, widespread, and essential; many plants struggle to grow without it. It is also well established that a single fungus can connect to the roots of more than one plant, creating what researchers call a common mycorrhizal network.", "The more dramatic claims rest on further steps. In the 1990s, field experiments using labeled carbon suggested that carbon could move from one tree to another tree of a different species, apparently through fungal connections. The findings, published in a prominent journal under the memorable 'wood wide web' label, launched decades of research and, eventually, an extraordinary wave of public enthusiasm. Over time, the story told in popular accounts grew more confident and more emotional than the data themselves.", "In 2023, a group of researchers published a review that examined the field evidence behind three widely repeated claims: that common mycorrhizal networks are widespread in forests, that resources moving through them meaningfully benefit seedlings, and that mature trees preferentially send resources or warning signals to their offspring. They concluded that all three claims were far less supported than their popularity suggested. Few field studies had definitively shown networks connecting trees throughout a forest. Where carbon did move between trees, the amounts were often small, and it was frequently unclear whether it traveled through fungal links or by other routes through the soil. Evidence for preferential care of offspring was, in their assessment, largely absent from field studies. They also found that published papers sometimes cited earlier work as supporting conclusions that the original studies had not actually reached.", "It is important to be precise about what this critique does and does not say. It does not deny that mycorrhizal fungi are vital to forests, nor that fungi can link plants together. It does not prove that trees never share resources through fungal networks. Its claim is narrower and, in a way, more interesting: that a set of hypotheses, some of them plausible and worth pursuing, had been presented to the public as settled fact.", "How does such a gap open? Part of the answer lies in the appeal of the story itself. The image of a cooperative forest, full of generous elders and communicating neighbors, is beautiful, and it echoes human values that many people find comforting. Metaphors such as 'mother tree' and 'wood wide web' make complex biology memorable, but they also carry assumptions, such as intention and family loyalty, that the evidence may not support. Once a metaphor takes hold, each new finding tends to be read through it.", "None of this means scientists should avoid vivid language. Metaphors are among the most powerful tools for explaining science, and a public that cares about forests is better than one that does not. But a metaphor is a hypothesis in disguise, and it deserves the same scrutiny. The forest floor remains one of the least understood environments on Earth, and the real relationships between fungi and trees are likely to be stranger and more complicated than any single story. That complexity is not a disappointment. It is an invitation to keep looking."], "q": [["The main purpose of the passage is to", ["prove that trees cannot communicate in any way and that fungi play no role in forest health", "argue that forests should not be studied by the public", "praise popular books for teaching forest science accurately and responsibly to millions of readers around the world", "distinguish the well-supported science of mycorrhizal networks from popular claims that go beyond the evidence"], 3, "The author separates the 'solid' foundation from 'more dramatic claims' that were 'presented to the public as settled fact.'"], ["The second paragraph primarily serves to", ["establish the aspects of the topic that are not in dispute", "introduce the idea of mother trees and their relationship with seedlings", "criticize researchers from the 1990s for their careless methods", "explain how photosynthesis works inside the fungi themselves"], 0, "It begins 'The foundation of the idea is solid' and describes well-established plant-fungus partnerships."], ["According to the passage, the 2023 review found that", ["fungal networks do not exist", "carbon never moves between trees under any conditions, whether through fungal links or through the soil itself", "evidence that mature trees preferentially help their offspring was largely absent from field studies", "seedlings cannot survive without mother trees"], 2, "The reviewers said evidence for preferential care of offspring was 'largely absent from field studies.'"], ["As used in the seventh paragraph, 'scrutiny' most nearly means", ["public attention", "careful examination", "official approval", "harsh punishment"], 1, "A metaphor 'deserves the same scrutiny' as a hypothesis, meaning it should be carefully tested."], ["Which statement about scientific metaphors would the author most likely agree with?", ["They should be banned from all serious science writing because they always mislead readers.", "They are always misleading.", "They can make science memorable but may carry assumptions that have not been proven.", "They are only useful for teaching children."], 2, "Metaphors 'make complex biology memorable, but they also carry assumptions... that the evidence may not support.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n8", "lv": 10, "e": "🛞", "t": "Counter-Steering", "p": ["The instructor's name was Ray, and he opened the course by announcing that everything the students believed about steering was wrong. 'You think you turn a motorcycle by turning the handlebars,' he said. 'That's how you turn a bicycle in a parking lot. At speed, you turn a motorcycle by pushing on the grip on the side you want to go. Push right, go right. It will feel backwards. Your brain will fight you. Your brain is not in charge today. I am.'", "Joanne, who was forty-seven and had signed up for the course during what her sister called 'an episode' and what Joanne preferred to call 'September,' wrote PUSH RIGHT GO RIGHT on the back of her hand in ballpoint pen. The other students were mostly men in their twenties who had arrived on loud machines and looked at the small training bikes the way a chef might look at a toy oven. There was also a retired dentist named Arturo, who asked a great many questions about liability.", "Joanne had not ridden anything with an engine since a moped in college. She had spent the intervening twenty-five years driving a series of increasingly large vehicles full of increasingly large children, and the last of those children, her son Danny, had left for a university two states away three weeks earlier. The house had become very quiet. She had found herself standing in his room holding a single sock, and had decided, with the clarity of a person making a small mistake on purpose, that she needed to learn something that frightened her.", "Ray explained the physics while the students sat on the curb. When you press forward on the right grip, the front wheel turns briefly to the left, just a little. That small steer to the left causes the bike to lean to the right, and the lean is what carries the motorcycle into a right-hand curve. 'You don't steer the bike,' he said. 'You tip it. The wheel follows.' He drew it in the dust with his boot. It made sense on the ground. On the bike, it did not.", "On the first practice curve, Joanne pulled the handlebar toward the turn, the way every nerve in her body insisted she should, and the motorcycle stood up and drifted wide toward the orange cones. Ray did not shout. He simply held up his right hand and made a small pushing motion, like someone gently closing a door. On the next pass she pushed, and felt the bike lean, and felt, astonishingly, the turn happen almost by itself, as though the machine had been waiting for her to stop interfering.", "The second lesson was about eyes. Riders, Ray said, go where they look. If you stare at the pothole, you will hit the pothole. If you stare at the cone, you will run over the cone. 'Look through the turn,' he said. 'Look where you want to end up, not at what you're afraid of.' Arturo asked whether this principle had been tested in court. Ray said it had been tested in emergency rooms, which was more convincing.", "By the second afternoon, Joanne was carving the curve in smooth arcs, head turned toward the exit, a small pressure on the grip doing the work her whole body had once tried to do. At the break, she sat on the curb, looked at the smudged words on her hand, and thought, not for the first time that weekend, about Danny. For eighteen years she had steered him the way she had first tried to steer the motorcycle: by pulling him toward where she wanted him to go. It had worked when he was small and slow. It did not work at speed.", "That night she called him. She did not ask whether he was eating vegetables or had found the laundry room, both of which she very much wanted to know. She asked about his classes and then mostly listened. Near the end, he told her about a professor he admired and a path he was considering that she would never have chosen for him, and she felt the familiar urge to pull. Instead, she pushed, very gently, in the direction he was already leaning. 'That sounds right for you,' she said. There was a pause, and then her son's voice changed in a way she had not heard in months, easier, as though something had stopped fighting him.", "She passed the riding test the next morning with one point deducted for a slightly wide turn, which Ray said was 'within the range of human dignity.' Arturo passed too, after asking whether his score could be appealed. Joanne went home and washed her hand, and found that the ink had soaked in deeply enough that the words were still faintly there. She decided she did not mind."], "q": [["Which choice best describes a central development in the passage?", ["A woman fails a motorcycle course and decides to give up riding.", "A group of young riders learns to respect their instructor's strict rules after a retired dentist nearly causes an accident during the first practice.", "A woman adjusting to her son's departure learns a counterintuitive riding technique that reshapes how she approaches her relationship with him.", "A mother convinces her son to change his plans for college."], 2, "Joanne connects counter-steering to parenting and 'pushed, very gently, in the direction he was already leaning.'"], ["The description of the young men looking at the training bikes 'the way a chef might look at a toy oven' mainly suggests that they", ["are excited and eager to try out new equipment that they have never ridden before", "consider the small training motorcycles beneath their skill and dignity", "are afraid of the training motorcycles", "plan to cook lunch during the break"], 1, "They 'had arrived on loud machines,' and a chef would view a toy oven as unworthy of real skill."], ["The phrase 'with the clarity of a person making a small mistake on purpose' most strongly suggests that Joanne", ["knows her decision is somewhat impulsive but embraces it deliberately", "regrets signing up for the course and wishes she could get her money back", "believes the course will be dangerous", "was pressured into the course by her sister"], 0, "She calls it a 'mistake' yet chooses it with 'clarity,' deciding 'she needed to learn something that frightened her.'"], ["As used in the eighth paragraph, 'pull' most nearly refers to", ["the physical act of steering a motorcycle through a curve", "her son's growing attraction to a professor he admires", "a weak phone connection that keeps cutting in and out", "the impulse to steer Danny toward her own preferences"], 3, "Hearing about a path 'she would never have chosen for him,' she feels 'the familiar urge to pull,' echoing how she used to steer him."], ["Which choice best describes the structure of the passage?", ["A list of motorcycle safety rules followed by a test score", "A flashback to Joanne's college years that explains her fear of motorcycles", "A series of riding lessons that gradually become a parallel to a parenting challenge", "An argument about whether older adults should ride motorcycles"], 2, "Ray's lessons on pushing and looking ahead are later applied to how Joanne talks with Danny."]]},{"id": "r10_n9", "lv": 10, "e": "⚓", "t": "The Clock That Found the Sea", "p": ["For most of the history of ocean travel, a ship's navigator could answer one of the two great questions about location with confidence and the other only with a guess. Latitude, a ship's position north or south of the equator, could be found by measuring the height of the sun at noon or of certain stars at night. Longitude, the position east or west, was another matter. A sailor could know precisely how far north he was and have no reliable idea whether land lay a hundred miles ahead or already behind him.", "The difficulty was not a lack of cleverness but a feature of the planet. Because Earth rotates, there is no natural starting line for east and west, as the equator provides for north and south. What the rotation does provide is a relationship between distance and time. Earth turns a full 360 degrees in twenty-four hours, or fifteen degrees every hour. If a navigator knew the time at his home port at the very moment it was noon aboard ship, the difference between the two times would reveal how far east or west he had traveled. Four minutes of difference meant one degree of longitude. The problem of longitude, in other words, was a problem of keeping time.", "That was precisely what no one could do at sea. The best clocks of the seventeenth century relied on pendulums, which were useless on a pitching deck. Changes in temperature made metal parts expand and contract, and humidity thickened the oils that lubricated them. A clock that lost or gained even a minute a day would, over a long voyage, produce errors large enough to wreck a ship. And ships were wrecked. In 1707, a British fleet returning from the Mediterranean ran onto the rocks of the Isles of Scilly, off the southwestern tip of England, and well over a thousand sailors died. Navigational error was widely blamed.", "In 1714, the British Parliament passed the Longitude Act, offering a reward of up to twenty thousand pounds, an immense fortune at the time, for a practical method of determining longitude at sea. Many of the most eminent scientists of the age believed the answer would come from the sky, using the moon's changing position against the background stars as a kind of celestial clock. The method was sound in principle but required precise tables of the moon's motion and hours of difficult calculation.", "The most famous challenge to the astronomers came from an unlikely direction. John Harrison was a carpenter from Lincolnshire with no formal scientific education who had taught himself to build clocks, including early ones with gears made of wood. Over several decades he produced a series of marine timekeepers, each attacking the problems of motion, friction, and temperature with inventive solutions, such as strips of two different metals arranged so that their expansions would cancel out. His first three machines were large and intricate. His fourth, completed around 1759, was a surprise: a large watch only about five inches across. On a trial voyage to Jamaica in 1761 and 1762, it lost only about five seconds over roughly eighty days at sea.", "One might expect such a result to have ended the contest. It did not. The Board of Longitude, which administered the prize, demanded further trials, required Harrison to explain his methods in detail, and questioned whether his watch could be reproduced by other makers. Some of these demands were reasonable; a single remarkable device is not the same as a practical system. Others seem to have reflected a preference for the astronomical approach championed by several of the board's members. Harrison received substantial payments over the years, but it was only in 1773, when he was around eighty years old and after King George III himself took an interest in his cause, that Parliament granted him a further sum in recognition of his work.", "In the end, both approaches proved valuable, and navigators used lunar observations for decades while chronometers remained expensive. But as manufacturing improved, the marine chronometer became standard equipment on ships, and the problem of longitude, which had cost countless lives, became a matter of routine. The story is often told as a tale of a lone genius against an arrogant establishment, and it contains some truth. It also contains a less dramatic lesson: that a breakthrough is not complete when it works once, but only when it can be trusted, copied, and put into ordinary hands."], "q": [["Which choice best states the central idea of the passage?", ["Solving the longitude problem required a reliable way to keep time at sea, a challenge that took decades of invention and debate to resolve.", "Astronomers were entirely wrong about how to find longitude, and their lunar method never helped a single ship find its way across the ocean.", "The Isles of Scilly disaster was caused by a faulty clock.", "John Harrison was cheated out of any reward for his work."], 0, "The passage explains that 'the problem of longitude... was a problem of keeping time' and traces Harrison's decades of work and the board's debates."], ["The explanation in the second paragraph primarily serves to", ["describe in detail exactly how latitude is measured using the sun and the stars", "argue that Earth's rotation is irregular and slowly changes from year to year", "clarify why determining longitude depended on measuring time accurately", "explain why the equator was chosen as a starting line for east and west"], 2, "Because Earth turns 'fifteen degrees every hour,' a time difference reveals distance east or west."], ["The author mentions the 1707 disaster in the Isles of Scilly mainly to", ["show that the British navy of that time was poorly led by its admirals", "explain why pendulum clocks were invented in the first place", "prove that the Mediterranean Sea was especially dangerous for sailors", "illustrate the deadly consequences of navigational error"], 3, "It follows the statement that timing errors could 'wreck a ship. And ships were wrecked.'"], ["As used in the sixth paragraph, 'championed' most nearly means", ["defeated", "advocated", "invented", "won"], 1, "Board members 'championed,' or supported and promoted, the astronomical approach."], ["In the final paragraph, the author suggests that the popular version of Harrison's story", ["is completely false and should be completely forgotten by anyone who studies the history of navigation", "proves that institutions should never be trusted", "is partly true but overlooks the importance of making an invention reliable and reproducible", "exaggerates the accuracy of Harrison's fourth timekeeper"], 2, "The 'lone genius' tale 'contains some truth,' but a breakthrough is complete only 'when it can be trusted, copied, and put into ordinary hands.'"]]},{"id": "r10_n10", "lv": 10, "e": "🌡️", "t": "The Thermostat", "p": ["The thermostat on the third floor of the Pemberton Insurance building was the most beloved object in the company. It was beige and rectangular, mounted beside the water cooler, and it had a small dial that turned from sixty-five to seventy-eight degrees with a satisfying click. Employees adjusted it constantly. Linda from claims turned it up every morning at 8:05. Doug from underwriting turned it down every morning at 8:20. Others visited throughout the day, turning, clicking, sighing with relief, and returning to their desks, where they reported feeling noticeably better.", "The thermostat was not connected to anything. Wen discovered this in her second week as assistant facilities coordinator, when she opened the cover to replace what she assumed was a dead battery and found no battery, no wires, and a small handwritten label that said, in the careful block capitals of her supervisor, Gerald Pruitt: THANK YOU FOR YOUR INTEREST.", "She brought the label to Gerald's office, which was in the basement next to the actual climate control system, a humming gray cabinet that kept the entire building at seventy-one degrees and had not been adjusted in years. Gerald was sixty-four, wore a cardigan in every season, and had worked at Pemberton for thirty-three years. He looked at the label as though greeting an old friend.", "'Ah,' he said. 'You've met the dial.'", "Wen explained that she had concerns. It seemed, she said, a little dishonest. People believed they were controlling the temperature. They were making decisions based on false information.", "Gerald nodded slowly. He told her that years ago, before the dial, the third floor had filed forty-one temperature complaints in a single quarter. Two people had stopped speaking to each other over a window. A man in accounting had brought in a space heater that tripped the breaker and erased an afternoon of work. Gerald had tried reasoning, memos, and a meeting with a flip chart. None of it helped. Then he installed the dial. In the following quarter, complaints fell to three, and one of those was about the carpet.", "'They're not upset about the temperature,' he said. 'They're upset about not having any say. The dial gives them a say.'", "'A fake say,' said Wen.", "'A small one,' said Gerald, as though that settled the matter. 'The temperature doesn't change. The feeling does. And the feeling was the complaint.'", "Wen was not convinced, and she spent the following weeks in a state of mild ethical discomfort, watching Linda turn the dial up and Doug turn it down with the satisfied air of people who believed they were winning a long war. She noticed that the two of them had begun to joke about it. Doug had taped a small paper flag reading DOUG'S TERRITORY near the sixty-eight mark. Linda had replaced it with a flag of her own. Others joined in, and a friendly rivalry formed around the water cooler, conducted entirely through a device that did nothing whatsoever.", "Gerald retired in the spring. On his last day, he handed Wen a single key on a ring. It opened the gray cabinet in the basement. 'You'll make your own decisions,' he said. 'That's the job.'", "Wen thought about it for a long time. In the end, she did something Gerald had never done: she went down to the basement, unlocked the gray cabinet, and lowered the building's temperature by one degree, because the utility bills suggested that seventy would save the company real money without making anyone measurably less comfortable. Nobody noticed. The next morning, Linda turned the dial up. Doug turned it down. Both of them said the office felt perfect.", "Wen left the dial where it was. She did, however, replace Gerald's label with one of her own. It read: THANK YOU FOR YOUR INTEREST. YOUR OPINION HAS BEEN RECORDED. Then, after a moment's reflection, she opened a notebook in the basement, made the first tally mark, and began, for the first time in the dial's long career, to actually record them."], "q": [["Which choice best describes a central development in the passage?", ["A longtime supervisor is fired after angry employees discover that the thermostat on their floor was fake all along.", "Two coworkers end their long feud after learning the truth about the thermostat from the new employee.", "A company replaces its heating system to save money.", "A new employee, troubled by a fake thermostat, comes to understand its purpose and finds her own way to handle it."], 3, "Wen moves from calling the dial 'dishonest' to keeping it while quietly adding a real record of opinions."], ["The label reading 'THANK YOU FOR YOUR INTEREST' primarily serves to", ["show that Gerald honestly did not know the thermostat was broken and needed a new battery", "reveal with dry humor that the thermostat was deliberately designed as a placebo", "explain how to install a new battery", "prove that employees had complained in writing"], 1, "The polite message hidden inside an unwired device shows that someone installed it on purpose, and the phrasing is comically bureaucratic."], ["Gerald's account of the complaints from years earlier serves mainly to", ["support his claim that the dial solved a real workplace problem", "show that the building's heating system was broken", "criticize the employees for being childish", "explain why he is retiring"], 0, "Complaints fell from forty-one to three after the dial was installed, 'and one of those was about the carpet.'"], ["As used in the seventh paragraph, 'say' most nearly means", ["a spoken sentence", "a famous quotation", "a voice in a decision", "a legal right"], 2, "Gerald believes employees are upset 'about not having any say,' meaning no input into what happens."], ["Wen's final action of recording the tally marks most strongly suggests that she", ["plans to report Gerald to management for deceiving the employees on the third floor for so many years", "wants to give employees' preferences some real weight while keeping the dial's benefits", "has decided the dial should be removed", "is secretly on Linda's side in the rivalry"], 1, "She keeps the dial but changes the label to 'YOUR OPINION HAS BEEN RECORDED' and then actually records the opinions."]]});
