window.MORE_STORIES.push({"id": "r2_x1", "lv": 2, "e": "🐿️", "t": "The Forgetful Squirrel", "p": ["In the fall, Red the squirrel was very busy. She dug small holes all over the park. In each hole she hid an acorn for winter.", "When the snow came, Red was hungry. She sniffed and dug, sniffed and dug. She found many acorns, but she forgot where some of them were.", "In the spring, tiny green sprouts popped up in the park. The lost acorns had turned into baby oak trees! Red did not know it, but she was a tree planter."], "q": [["What is this story mostly about?", ["How a squirrel's lost acorns grow into trees", "A squirrel who is scared of the snow all winter", "A park where no trees can ever grow", "How to make a snack from acorns"], 0, "Red hides acorns, forgets some, and they grow into oak trees."], ["Why did Red hide acorns in the fall?", ["To play a hiding game with birds", "To keep them dry", "To give them to a friend", "To save food for winter"], 3, "\"In each hole she hid an acorn for winter.\""], ["What grew from the acorns Red forgot?", ["Oak trees", "Tall grass", "Apple trees", "Flowers"], 0, "\"The lost acorns had turned into baby oak trees!\""]]},{"id": "r2_x2", "lv": 2, "e": "🏖️", "t": "The Sandcastle Team", "p": ["Leo wanted to build the biggest sandcastle on the beach. He dug and patted all by himself. But every time a wave came, his wall fell down.", "A girl named Rosa was filling a bucket nearby. 'I can carry water and wet sand,' she said. 'Wet sand sticks better.' Leo thought about it. 'Okay,' he said. 'Let's build it together.'", "Rosa carried the wet sand, and Leo made the towers. They dug a deep moat to catch the waves. At the end of the day, their castle was still standing tall."], "q": [["What is the lesson of the story?", ["Working together can solve a problem", "Beaches are much too wet to build castles", "Waves are fun to jump in", "Towers should always be tall"], 0, "Leo could not do it alone, but with Rosa's help the castle stood."], ["Why did Rosa say to use wet sand?", ["It is warmer in the sun", "It is easier to carry", "It is a nicer color", "It sticks better"], 3, "\"Wet sand sticks better.\""], ["What did they dig to catch the waves?", ["A long tunnel", "A moat", "A pool", "A cave"], 1, "\"They dug a deep moat to catch the waves.\""]]},{"id": "r4_x1", "lv": 4, "e": "🦇", "t": "Seeing with Sound", "p": ["When the sun goes down, a small brown bat leaves its roost under a bridge. The sky is almost dark, but the bat zips and swerves without bumping into a single branch. How does it do it?", "Many bats use a skill called echolocation. As the bat flies, it makes quick, high squeaks, most of them too high for people to hear. The sound bounces off whatever is in front of it and comes back as an echo. By listening to the echoes, the bat can tell how far away something is, how big it is, and even which way it is moving.", "This skill makes bats amazing hunters. A single little brown bat can catch hundreds of insects, such as moths, beetles and mosquitoes, in one night. Farmers are glad, because many of those insects would otherwise eat their crops.", "Some bats help in other ways. In the deserts of Mexico and the southwestern United States, nectar-eating bats visit agave and cactus flowers at night. As they drink, they carry pollen from flower to flower, helping new seeds form.", "Bats may look spooky, but they are some of the most helpful animals in the night sky."], "q": [["What is the main idea of this passage?", ["Bats are spooky animals that people should stay away from", "Bats use echolocation and help people and plants", "Bats live only in deserts", "Mosquitoes are the most common insect"], 1, "The passage explains echolocation and how bats help farmers and flowers."], ["How does echolocation work?", ["The bat's eyes see perfectly well in total darkness", "The bat smells its food", "The bat follows other bats", "The bat listens for echoes of its own squeaks"], 3, "\"The sound bounces off whatever is in front of it and comes back as an echo.\""], ["Why are farmers glad to have bats nearby?", ["Bats pull weeds out of the fields", "Bats eat insects that harm crops", "Bats scare away hungry deer at night", "Bats water the plants"], 1, "Bats eat insects that \"would otherwise eat their crops.\""], ["In this passage, the word 'roost' means", ["a kind of flying insect", "a place where bats rest", "a loud squeak", "a desert flower that opens at night"], 1, "The bat \"leaves its roost under a bridge\" when it goes out to hunt, so a roost is its resting place."]]},{"id": "r4_x2", "lv": 4, "e": "🧭", "t": "Sacagawea on the Trail", "p": ["In 1804, President Thomas Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore the lands west of the Mississippi River. Their group was called the Corps of Discovery. They hoped to reach the Pacific Ocean.", "That winter, in what is now North Dakota, they hired a French-Canadian trader named Toussaint Charbonneau. His young wife, Sacagawea, came too. She was a Shoshone woman who had been taken from her people years before. In February 1805 she gave birth to a baby boy, and she carried him on her back for the rest of the journey.", "Sacagawea helped the explorers in many ways. She found roots and berries to eat. Once, when a boat nearly tipped over, she calmly rescued journals and supplies from the water. And when the group reached Shoshone land, she translated for them. To everyone's surprise, the Shoshone chief turned out to be her own brother! Thanks to her, the explorers were able to trade for the horses they needed to cross the mountains.", "The Corps of Discovery reached the Pacific in November 1805. Today, rivers, schools and even a U.S. dollar coin honor Sacagawea."], "q": [["What is this passage mostly about?", ["How explorers traded with Native nations for horses and boats", "Why Jefferson became President", "How Sacagawea helped the Lewis and Clark journey", "Life in North Dakota today"], 2, "The passage lists the many ways Sacagawea helped the Corps of Discovery."], ["Why were the explorers able to get horses from the Shoshone?", ["They paid the Shoshone with gold and silver coins", "Sacagawea translated; the chief was her brother", "They found wild horses", "Clark had once lived with the Shoshone people"], 1, "She translated, the chief was her brother, and \"thanks to her\" they traded for horses."], ["What does Sacagawea's rescue of the journals suggest about her?", ["She never learned how to swim in deep water", "She wanted the journals", "She stayed calm in an emergency", "She was afraid of boats"], 2, "\"She calmly rescued journals and supplies from the water.\""], ["When did the Corps of Discovery reach the Pacific Ocean?", ["November 1805", "February 1805", "In the spring of 1804", "They never did"], 0, "\"The Corps of Discovery reached the Pacific in November 1805.\""]]},{"id": "r4_x3", "lv": 4, "e": "⚾", "t": "The Tag Nobody Saw", "p": ["It was the last inning of the kickball game at recess, and the score was tied. Mateo kicked the big red ball deep into the outfield and raced around the bases. As he slid toward home plate, Ella reached out with the ball and brushed his sneaker.", "'Safe!' yelled Dev, who was acting as the umpire. Mateo's team leaped up and cheered. They had won!", "But Mateo did not cheer. He had felt the ball touch his shoe. Nobody else had seen it, not even Dev. Mateo looked at Ella, who was staring at the ground. Her team had played hard all week.", "Mateo took a deep breath. 'Dev,' he said, 'she tagged me. I felt it. I'm out.' For a second, nobody said anything. Then Ella grinned. 'Thanks, Mateo,' she said. 'Tomorrow's game will decide it, then.'", "The next day, Ella picked Mateo first for her team."], "q": [["What is the theme of this story?", ["Being honest matters more than winning", "Winning the game is all that really matters", "Umpires are never wrong", "Kickball is better than baseball"], 0, "Mateo gives up the win because he knows he was tagged."], ["How did Mateo know he was out?", ["He felt the ball touch his shoe", "Dev told him", "Ella shouted it across the whole field", "He saw it on video"], 0, "\"He had felt the ball touch his shoe.\""], ["Why did Ella most likely pick Mateo first the next day?", ["He was the fastest runner in the class", "She trusted and respected him", "Dev told her to", "Nobody else wanted him"], 1, "His honesty earned her respect, so she wanted him on her team."], ["What does it mean that Ella 'brushed' his sneaker?", ["She lightly touched it", "She wiped it clean with her hand", "She kicked it hard", "She missed it"], 0, "The ball only barely touched his shoe, which is why no one else saw it."]]},{"id": "r4_x4", "lv": 4, "e": "🌲", "t": "Hug a Tree", "p": ["Camila and her dad were hiking the Pine Loop Trail when she stopped to look at a bright orange mushroom. When she looked up, Dad was gone. The trail had split in two, and she could not remember which way they had come.", "Her heart pounded. Her first thought was to run and find him. Then she remembered what the ranger had said at the start of the hike: 'If you get lost, stop. Stay where you are. Hug a tree.'", "Camila found a big pine near the trail and sat down beside it. She took the whistle from her backpack and blew three long blasts, the signal for help. She put on her jacket, drank some water, and waited. Every few minutes, she blew the whistle again.", "Before long she heard an answer: two short whistles and her dad's voice calling her name. He had turned back as soon as he noticed she was missing. 'You did exactly the right thing,' he said, hugging her. 'If you had wandered, it could have taken hours to find you.'"], "q": [["What is the most important lesson of this story?", ["Mushrooms are dangerous to touch on a hike", "Always hike alone", "If you get lost, stay put and signal for help", "Pine trees are the best trees to climb when lost"], 2, "Camila stays in one place and uses her whistle, and her dad finds her quickly."], ["What signal did Camila use to call for help?", ["She yelled one time", "She waved a bright flag", "Three long whistle blasts", "She lit a small smoky campfire"], 2, "\"She blew three long blasts, the signal for help.\""], ["Why did Dad say staying put was the right thing?", ["A moving person is harder to find", "It kept her warm", "Hiking is too tiring for kids", "The trail was closed for the night"], 0, "\"If you had wandered, it could have taken hours to find you.\""], ["How did Camila feel when she first noticed she was lost?", ["Bored", "Scared", "Sleepy", "Proud"], 1, "\"Her heart pounded,\" which shows she was frightened."]]},{"id": "r5_x1", "lv": 5, "e": "🐝", "t": "The Dance of the Honeybees", "p": ["A honeybee scout has just found a field of blooming clover more than a mile from her hive. She cannot carry all that nectar home by herself, and she cannot speak. So how does she tell thousands of sisters where to go? She dances.", "Back inside the dark hive, the scout climbs onto the wall of honeycomb and runs a short, straight line while shaking her body from side to side. This is called the waggle run. Then she circles back and does it again, over and over. The other bees crowd around and follow her with their antennae.", "Every part of the dance is a message. The direction of the waggle run shows which way to fly compared to the sun. If she waggles straight up the comb, the food is toward the sun. If she waggles a little to the right, the food is a little to the right of the sun. The length of the waggle shows the distance: the longer she waggles, the farther away the flowers are. The bees also smell the flower scent on her body, which helps them know what to look for.", "An Austrian scientist named Karl von Frisch spent years watching bees at glass-walled hives to crack this code. In 1973 he shared a Nobel Prize for his work. Today, scientists still study the waggle dance as one of the most remarkable ways animals share information."], "q": [["What is the main idea of this passage?", ["Honeybees make honey from clover and other flowers", "Karl von Frisch was an Austrian scientist", "Bees dance to tell other bees where food is", "Bee hives are dark inside"], 2, "The passage explains how the waggle dance tells other bees the direction and distance to food."], ["What does the length of the waggle run tell the other bees?", ["How sweet the nectar is", "What color the flowers are", "How far away the flowers are", "How many bees should fly out"], 2, "\"The longer she waggles, the farther away the flowers are.\""], ["If a scout waggles straight up the honeycomb, where is the food?", ["Toward the sun", "Away from the sun", "Right under the hive", "Inside the hive"], 0, "\"If she waggles straight up the comb, the food is toward the sun.\""], ["In this passage, 'crack this code' means", ["break open the honeycomb", "write a secret message to other bees", "stop the bees from dancing", "figure out what the dance means"], 3, "Von Frisch studied the dance for years to understand its hidden meaning."]]},{"id": "r5_x2", "lv": 5, "e": "🚂", "t": "The Golden Spike", "p": ["In the 1850s, getting from New York to California was a long, hard trip. Travelers could spend months crossing the country in a covered wagon, or sail all the way around South America. Many people dreamed of a railroad that would stretch from coast to coast.", "In 1863, two companies began to build it. The Central Pacific started in Sacramento, California, and built east. Its crews had to blast tunnels through the granite of the Sierra Nevada, often in deep snow. Most of those workers were immigrants from China, who did some of the most dangerous work on the whole line. Meanwhile, the Union Pacific started near Omaha, Nebraska, and built west across the plains. Many of its workers were Irish immigrants and soldiers who had just fought in the Civil War.", "On May 10, 1869, the two lines finally met at Promontory Summit in Utah Territory. Leaders tapped a ceremonial golden spike into the last tie. A telegraph operator sent a one-word message across the nation: DONE.", "The railroad changed America. A trip that had taken months now took about a week. But it also brought great harm to many Native American nations, as settlers and hunters poured onto their lands and the huge buffalo herds were nearly wiped out."], "q": [["What is this passage mostly about?", ["The building of the first railroad across the country", "Daily life for families traveling west in a covered wagon", "How telegraphs send messages", "Why gold spikes are valuable"], 0, "It tells how two companies built the line and what happened when it was finished."], ["Where did the two railroad lines meet?", ["Sacramento, California", "Omaha, Nebraska", "New York City, New York", "Promontory Summit, Utah"], 3, "\"The two lines finally met at Promontory Summit in Utah Territory.\""], ["Why was building through the Sierra Nevada so hard?", ["There was no clean water anywhere to drink", "The ground was too soft", "The mountains were too flat to build on", "Crews had to blast granite, often in snow"], 3, "Crews \"had to blast tunnels through the granite of the Sierra Nevada, often in deep snow.\""], ["Which sentence best shows the railroad had both good and bad effects?", ["The very last spike hammered into the track was made of gold.", "Travel got faster, but Native nations were harmed.", "Workers came from many places.", "The telegraph message said DONE."], 1, "The last paragraph gives a benefit (a week instead of months) and a harm (to Native American nations)."]]},{"id": "r5_x3", "lv": 5, "e": "🧩", "t": "The Quiet Idea", "p": ["When Ms. Park announced the bridge-building challenge, Sofia, Marcus and Eli ended up in the same group. Their job was to build a bridge from craft sticks and glue that could hold as many textbooks as possible.", "Sofia and Marcus started talking right away. They wanted a tall arch with towers on each end, like a famous bridge they had seen in a movie. Eli sketched quietly in his notebook. Once, he said, 'What if we use triangles?' but Marcus was already explaining his tower plan, and nobody heard.", "The arch bridge looked amazing. But during the practice test, it bent and snapped under just two books. Marcus groaned. Sofia sighed. Eli slowly turned his notebook around. It was full of drawings of bridges made of triangles, with notes like 'triangles don't change shape when you push on them.'", "Sofia looked at the page for a long moment. 'Eli, why didn't you say something?' 'I tried,' Eli said with a shrug. Marcus's face turned red. 'Sorry,' he said. 'Show us how it works.'", "Their new triangle bridge held eleven books, the most in the class. Afterward, Sofia made a new group rule: before anyone votes on a plan, everyone gets a turn to talk."], "q": [["What is the theme of the story?", ["Tall bridges with towers are always the strongest", "Craft sticks are hard to glue", "The loudest person should lead", "Good ideas can come from quiet people"], 3, "Eli's ignored idea turns out to be the best one, and the group changes how it listens."], ["Why did nobody hear Eli's first idea?", ["He whispered it only to Ms. Park", "He wrote it on the board", "Marcus was busy explaining his own plan", "Sofia told him to be quiet and let others talk"], 2, "\"Marcus was already explaining his tower plan, and nobody heard.\""], ["Why did Marcus's face turn red?", ["He was angry at Eli for hiding the notebook", "He was too hot", "He was embarrassed that he hadn't listened", "He had been running"], 2, "He realizes Eli had tried to share and apologizes: 'Sorry.'"], ["Why is the group's new rule a good solution?", ["It makes the group meetings much shorter and quicker", "It gives every member a chance to share ideas", "It lets Sofia choose every plan", "It means they never vote"], 1, "Everyone getting a turn prevents a good idea like Eli's from being missed."]]},{"id": "r5_x4", "lv": 5, "e": "⚡", "t": "Counting to Thunder", "p": ["The soccer game was tied 1–1 when Coach Ramos blew her whistle and waved both teams off the field. The sky overhead was still blue, and some players groaned. 'But it isn't even raining!' said Tyler.", "'Look west,' Coach said. A dark wall of clouds was building over the hills. As they watched, a thin flash of lightning flickered inside it. Coach began to count out loud. 'One, two, three...' When she reached fifteen, a low rumble of thunder rolled across the field.", "'Light travels much faster than sound,' she explained as everyone walked toward the school. 'We see the flash almost instantly, but the sound of thunder takes about five seconds to travel one mile. Fifteen seconds means that lightning was about three miles away.'", "'Three miles is far,' Tyler said. Coach shook her head. 'Lightning can strike ten miles or more from a storm, even where the sky looks clear. That's why we say: When thunder roars, go indoors.'", "Inside the gym, the players watched rain sheet down the windows. Coach made them wait until thirty minutes had passed without any thunder before anyone went back outside. The game finished the next day, and nobody complained about the wait."], "q": [["What is the main purpose of this story?", ["To explain the rules of soccer and how a tie game works", "To show that Tyler is a bad player", "To teach how to judge and stay safe from lightning", "To describe a school gym"], 2, "Coach explains how to estimate lightning's distance and why to go indoors."], ["Why do we see lightning before we hear thunder?", ["Light travels much faster than sound", "Thunder is much quieter than lightning", "Our eyes are closer than our ears", "Thunder happens later"], 0, "\"Light travels much faster than sound.\""], ["If thunder comes 10 seconds after a flash, about how far away is the lightning?", ["1 mile", "2 miles", "5 miles", "10 miles"], 1, "Sound takes about five seconds per mile, so 10 seconds is about 2 miles."], ["Why did Coach stop the game when the sky above was still blue?", ["Lightning can strike miles from a storm", "The other team asked her to stop the game", "It was time for lunch", "The field was too muddy"], 0, "\"Lightning can strike ten miles or more from a storm, even where the sky looks clear.\""]]},{"id": "r6_x1", "lv": 6, "e": "🌊", "t": "The Moon's Tug on the Sea", "p": ["If you spend a whole day at the beach, you may notice something strange. In the morning, the waves might crash near your towel. By afternoon, the water has pulled back, leaving a wide stretch of wet sand, tide pools and stranded seaweed. A few hours later, the sea creeps back again. This steady rise and fall of the ocean is called the tide, and its main cause is about 239,000 miles away: the Moon.", "Every object with mass pulls on every other object. This pull is called gravity. The Moon's gravity tugs on Earth, and because water can move freely, the oceans respond. The water on the side of Earth facing the Moon is pulled a little toward it, forming a bulge. A second bulge forms on the opposite side of the planet, where the Moon's pull is weakest. As Earth spins, most coastlines pass through both bulges each day, which is why many beaches have two high tides and two low tides about every twenty-four hours and fifty minutes.", "The Sun affects tides too, though less than the Moon does, because it is so much farther away. When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up during a new moon or full moon, their pulls combine. The result is a spring tide, with extra-high high tides and extra-low low tides. (The name has nothing to do with the season.) When the Sun and Moon pull at right angles, during a quarter moon, the tides are gentler. These are called neap tides.", "The shape of the coast matters as well. In Canada's Bay of Fundy, the narrow, funnel-shaped bay squeezes the incoming water so much that the difference between high and low tide can be more than forty-five feet, among the biggest tides in the world."], "q": [["What is the main idea of this passage?", ["Beaches are best to visit early in the morning", "The Bay of Fundy is in Canada", "Tides are caused mainly by the Moon's gravity", "Spring tides happen only in spring"], 2, "The passage explains how the Moon's gravity (with help from the Sun) creates tides."], ["Why do many coasts have two high tides a day?", ["Earth spins through two bulges of water", "The Moon circles all the way around Earth twice a day", "The Sun rises and sets", "The wind changes direction"], 0, "\"As Earth spins, most coastlines pass through both bulges each day.\""], ["When do spring tides happen?", ["When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up", "Only during the spring season each year", "During a quarter moon", "When the wind is at its strongest"], 0, "\"When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up during a new moon or full moon... The result is a spring tide.\""], ["Why are the Bay of Fundy's tides so large?", ["The funnel-shaped bay squeezes the water", "The Moon is much closer to Canada than to other places", "The water there is extra salty", "The bay has no waves"], 0, "\"The narrow, funnel-shaped bay squeezes the incoming water.\""]]},{"id": "r6_x2", "lv": 6, "e": "⭐", "t": "Harriet Tubman's Road North", "p": ["Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Dorchester County, Maryland, around 1822. As a child, she was hired out to work for other families, checking muskrat traps in icy marshes and doing heavy field work. When she was a teenager, an overseer threw a heavy metal weight at another enslaved person. It struck Tubman in the head instead. For the rest of her life she suffered from headaches and sudden sleeping spells.", "In 1849, afraid she would be sold away from her family, Tubman escaped. Traveling mostly at night and following the North Star, she walked about ninety miles to Pennsylvania, where slavery was illegal. 'When I found I had crossed that line,' she later said, 'I looked at my hands to see if I was the same person.'", "Freedom was not enough for her. Over the next ten years, Tubman returned to Maryland about thirteen times to guide others north along the Underground Railroad, a secret network of routes and safe houses. She led about seventy people to freedom, including her parents and several of her brothers. She often traveled in winter, when nights were long, and she started escapes on Saturday nights, because notices about runaways could not be printed in newspapers until Monday. She later said proudly that she never lost a passenger.", "During the Civil War, Tubman served the Union Army as a nurse, scout and spy. In 1863 she helped lead a raid along South Carolina's Combahee River that freed more than seven hundred enslaved people. She spent her later years in Auburn, New York, caring for elderly and poor people until her death in 1913."], "q": [["What is the passage mostly about?", ["The long history of Maryland's farms, marshes and rivers", "Harriet Tubman's courage in escaping and helping others", "How newspapers were printed", "Life in Auburn, New York"], 1, "It follows Tubman from her escape through her work on the Underground Railroad and in the war."], ["Why did Tubman often begin escapes on Saturday nights?", ["Runaway notices couldn't be printed until Monday", "Enslavers slept late on Sundays and would not notice", "The moon was brightest then", "Trains ran only on weekends"], 0, "\"Notices about runaways could not be printed in newspapers until Monday.\""], ["What does her quote about looking at her hands suggest?", ["Freedom felt so new it was hard to believe", "She had badly hurt her hands on the long walk", "She was lost and confused", "She wanted to go back"], 0, "She felt so changed by freedom she checked whether she was 'the same person.'"], ["In this passage, the Underground Railroad was", ["a train that ran through long tunnels under the ground", "a Union Army camp", "a newspaper for runaways", "a secret network of routes and safe houses"], 3, "The passage defines it as \"a secret network of routes and safe houses.\""]]},{"id": "r6_x3", "lv": 6, "e": "🎨", "t": "Two Painters, One Wall", "p": ["When the principal announced that the sixth grade would paint a mural on the long wall beside the cafeteria, Hana knew exactly what it should look like: an underwater scene, full of sea turtles, glowing jellyfish and schools of silver fish. She had been sketching ocean animals since she was six.", "The trouble was that Diego also knew exactly what it should look like, and his idea was completely different. He wanted a giant city skyline at sunset, with a train running across the bottom and tiny people waving from the windows. When the class voted, the result was a perfect tie, thirteen to thirteen.", "For two days, Hana and Diego barely spoke. At lunch, each of them sat with their own supporters and complained. 'His idea is so boring,' Hana said. 'Hers is so babyish,' Diego said at the next table, loud enough for her to hear.", "On the third day, the art teacher, Mr. Obi, asked them both to stay after class. He did not lecture them. He simply rolled out a long strip of butcher paper and handed each of them a pencil. 'You have twenty minutes,' he said. 'One drawing. Both of you.'", "At first they drew on opposite ends, not looking at each other. Then Diego noticed that Hana's ocean needed a shoreline. He drew a pier stretching into the water. Hana added a lighthouse at the end of it and a harbor full of boats. Diego's city grew along the harbor's edge, and his train crossed a bridge above Hana's turtles.", "When the mural was finished a month later, visitors always asked the same question: who designed it? Hana and Diego always gave the same answer, at the same time: 'We did.'"], "q": [["What is the theme of this story?", ["School murals should always show the ocean and its animals", "Votes should never end in a tie", "Teachers should choose the mural", "Combining different ideas can create something better"], 3, "Hana and Diego's joined design becomes a mural both are proud of."], ["Why did Mr. Obi give them one sheet of paper?", ["The school had run out of paper for the art room", "He wanted to test their drawing speed", "He liked Diego's idea better", "To make them work together on one design"], 3, "'One drawing. Both of you.' forces them to cooperate."], ["Which event is the turning point of the story?", ["The class vote ends in a tie", "Hana sits with her supporters at lunch", "The principal makes the announcement", "Diego draws a pier into Hana's ocean"], 3, "When Diego connects his city to her ocean, they start building on each other's ideas."], ["What does their answer 'We did' show at the end?", ["They still disagree about the design", "They are proud of working together", "They forgot who drew it", "They want no credit for the mural at all"], 1, "Answering together shows they now see the mural as a shared success."]]},{"id": "r6_x4", "lv": 6, "e": "🧗", "t": "Top Rope", "p": ["The climbing wall at the rec center was thirty feet tall and covered in bumpy, colorful holds. Kai had watched other kids scramble up it all summer. Now, strapped into a harness for his friend Tessa's birthday party, he was not sure his legs would move.", "The instructor, a woman named Bree with chalk on her fingers, checked his knot twice and tugged on the rope that ran up to an anchor at the top of the wall and back down to her belay device. 'I've got you the whole time,' she said. 'If you slip, the rope catches you. You won't fall more than a few inches.'", "Kai started up. The first ten feet were easy, like climbing a ladder. At fifteen feet, he made the mistake of looking down. The floor seemed impossibly far away. His hands began to sweat, and his arms started to shake. He pressed his body flat against the wall and froze.", "'Kai,' Bree called calmly. 'Don't look at the floor. Look at your feet. Find the green hold by your right knee.' He found it. 'Now push with your legs. Your legs are stronger than your arms.' He pushed, and he rose. One hold at a time, Bree talked him up the wall.", "Twenty-five feet. Twenty-eight. Then his hand slapped the bell at the top, and the clang echoed through the gym. Everyone cheered. When Bree lowered him back down, Kai's legs were wobbly, but he was grinning. 'Can I go again?' he asked.", "This time he did not look at the floor even once."], "q": [["What is the main conflict in this story?", ["Kai and Tessa have an argument at the party", "The rope breaks halfway up", "Bree forgets to check the knot on his harness", "Kai must overcome his fear while climbing"], 3, "Kai freezes in fear at fifteen feet and has to push through it."], ["What advice helped Kai the most?", ["Close your eyes and climb as fast as you can", "Look at your feet and push with your legs", "Pull only with your arms", "Look down to check the floor"], 1, "Bree told him to look at his feet and use his stronger legs."], ["Why did Bree tell Kai he would not fall more than a few inches?", ["To trick him into climbing higher than he wanted", "Because the wall was short", "To help him trust the rope and feel safe", "Because he wore a helmet"], 2, "She explains how the belay rope catches him so he can feel safe."], ["What does the last sentence show about how Kai has changed?", ["He has learned to stay focused on the climb", "He is now much more afraid of heights than before", "He wants to quit climbing", "He can no longer see the floor"], 0, "He follows Bree's advice on his own, showing new confidence."]]},{"id": "r7_x1", "lv": 7, "e": "🧬", "t": "Photograph 51", "p": ["In the early 1950s, some of the best scientists in the world were racing to solve one of biology's great mysteries. Many scientists had come to believe that DNA carried the instructions that pass from parents to children, but no one knew what the molecule actually looked like. Without its shape, no one could explain how it copied itself.", "At King's College London, a chemist named Rosalind Franklin was attacking the problem with a technique called X-ray crystallography. She fired X-rays through carefully prepared fibers of DNA and recorded how the rays scattered onto photographic film. Reading those patterns required patience and precise mathematics, and Franklin was known for both. She refused to guess at a structure until her data could support it.", "In May 1952, Franklin and her graduate student, Raymond Gosling, captured an unusually sharp image. It showed a bold X-shaped pattern of dark smudges. They labeled it Photograph 51. To a trained eye, that X was a strong clue that DNA was shaped like a helix, a twisting spiral.", "Franklin did not know that in January 1953 a colleague, Maurice Wilkins, showed the photograph to James Watson, a young American scientist working at Cambridge with Francis Crick. Watson later wrote that his 'mouth fell open' when he saw it. Using the image, along with measurements from a report on Franklin's research, Watson and Crick built their famous model of DNA as a double helix: two twisting strands connected like the rungs of a ladder. They published it in April 1953. Franklin's own paper, supporting the same structure, appeared in the same issue of the journal, printed after theirs.", "Franklin went on to do important research on viruses, but she died of cancer in 1958, at only thirty-seven. In 1962, Watson, Crick and Wilkins received the Nobel Prize for the discovery. Nobel Prizes are not given to people who have died, so Franklin could not be included. Today, many scientists argue that her careful work was one of the keys that unlocked the secret of life, and that she deserves far more credit than she received in her lifetime."], "q": [["What is the central idea of this passage?", ["Franklin's work was key to finding DNA's shape, but she got little credit", "Watson, Crick and Wilkins won a Nobel Prize in 1962 for their work on DNA", "X-rays can be dangerous to scientists", "DNA carries instructions from parents"], 0, "The passage shows how Photograph 51 led to the double helix and how Franklin was overlooked."], ["What did the X-shaped pattern in Photograph 51 suggest?", ["DNA was flat like a sheet of paper", "The camera was broken", "DNA was made of two X's", "DNA was shaped like a helix"], 3, "\"That X was a strong clue that DNA was shaped like a helix.\""], ["Which detail best shows that Franklin's work was used without her knowledge?", ["She later did important research on the structure of viruses.", "She worked at King's College London.", "Her paper appeared in the same issue.", "Wilkins showed the photo to Watson without telling her."], 3, "\"Franklin did not know that... Maurice Wilkins showed the photograph to James Watson.\""], ["Why was Franklin not included in the 1962 Nobel Prize?", ["Her photograph was later proven to be completely wrong", "She did not want it", "She was not a real scientist", "She had died, and it is not given after death"], 3, "\"Nobel Prizes are not given to people who have died.\""], ["As used in the passage, 'precise' most nearly means", ["quick and rough", "old-fashioned and slow", "expensive", "exact and careful"], 3, "Reading X-ray patterns required exact, careful mathematics."]]},{"id": "r7_x2", "lv": 7, "e": "🦠", "t": "The Mold on the Dish", "p": ["In September 1928, a Scottish scientist named Alexander Fleming returned to his crowded laboratory at St. Mary's Hospital in London after a summer vacation. Before leaving, he had stacked a pile of glass dishes in which he was growing Staphylococcus, bacteria that can cause serious infections. Now he began sorting through them, planning to wash them out.", "One dish made him stop. A blob of fuzzy mold had landed on it, probably drifting in from somewhere nearby. That alone was not unusual; molds were a common nuisance in labs. What caught Fleming's attention was the ring around the mold. In a wide circle, the bacteria had simply disappeared, as if something coming from the mold had killed them.", "Fleming identified the mold as a type of Penicillium and named the bacteria-killing substance it produced 'penicillin.' He tested it and found it could destroy many kinds of harmful bacteria. But he ran into a stubborn problem. Penicillin was hard to purify, and the mold made only tiny amounts. Fleming published his findings in 1929, but for about ten years the discovery attracted little attention.", "The breakthrough came at Oxford University, where a team led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain took up the challenge around 1939. They found ways to extract and purify penicillin, and in 1940 they showed that it could cure infected mice. Early human patients improved dramatically, but there was never enough of the drug to go around. During World War II, American and British companies worked together to grow the mold in huge tanks, and by 1944 there was enough penicillin to treat wounded soldiers on a large scale.", "In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize. Penicillin became the first widely used antibiotic, and it has saved millions of lives. Fleming himself warned, even then, that bacteria could become resistant if the drug were misused, a warning doctors still repeat today."], "q": [["What is the main idea of the passage?", ["Fleming liked to take long summer vacations away from his crowded lab", "Molds are always harmful in a lab", "World War II was fought in 1944", "A lucky observation led, with much more work, to penicillin"], 3, "The passage traces penicillin from Fleming's moldy dish to mass production."], ["What clue made Fleming notice the mold was special?", ["The bacteria around it had died", "It was a bright, unusual color", "It smelled strange", "It grew very fast across the whole dish"], 0, "\"In a wide circle, the bacteria had simply disappeared.\""], ["Why did the discovery attract little attention for about ten years?", ["Fleming kept his discovery a secret from other scientists for years", "Nobody believed molds existed", "It only worked on mice", "Penicillin was hard to purify and make in large amounts"], 3, "\"Penicillin was hard to purify, and the mold made only tiny amounts.\""], ["What can you infer about how science often works from this passage?", ["One scientist working alone usually makes every important discovery", "Discoveries often need many people and years of work", "Accidents are never useful", "Vacations lead to discoveries"], 1, "Fleming found it, but Florey, Chain and wartime companies were needed to make it useful."], ["In the passage, 'resistant' most nearly means", ["easy to see under a microscope", "able to survive the drug", "very small", "quick to spread"], 1, "Fleming warned bacteria could become able to withstand penicillin if it were misused."]]},{"id": "r7_x3", "lv": 7, "e": "🎭", "t": "The Understudy", "p": ["For six weeks, Maya had said every one of Juliet's lines under her breath. She had learned them sitting in the back row of the auditorium, while her best friend, Priya, stood in the spotlight and said them out loud. Maya was the understudy, which meant she memorized the whole part and then, almost certainly, never performed it.", "Two days before opening night, Priya called her, sounding like she had swallowed a cheese grater. 'Strep throat,' she croaked. 'The doctor says no talking for a week. You're on, Maya.'", "Maya felt two things at once, and she was ashamed of the first one. For one bright second she was thrilled. Then she heard Priya's voice crack, and she remembered how many nights her friend had rehearsed in her bedroom mirror, and the thrill curdled into guilt.", "The next two days were a blur of costume fittings and extra rehearsals. Priya texted her constantly: reminders about where to stand, a warning that the balcony wobbled, a list of lines she always stumbled on. On opening night, a bouquet arrived backstage with a card in Priya's messy handwriting: 'Break a leg. Not literally. It's your Juliet now.'", "Maya's hands shook until the moment she stepped into the light. Then, strangely, they stopped. She did not play Juliet exactly the way Priya had. She paused in different places and laughed at a line Priya had always said seriously. The audience laughed with her.", "At curtain call, Maya scanned the crowd and found a familiar face in the third row, wrapped in a scarf, clapping harder than anyone. Priya could not cheer, so she held up a sign instead. It said, in huge letters, MY FRIEND."], "q": [["What is the central conflict Maya faces?", ["She forgets all of her lines on opening night in front of everyone", "She wants to quit the play", "She and Priya argue about the role", "She's thrilled to perform but guilty about her sick friend"], 3, "Maya is thrilled at first, then feels guilty because the chance came from Priya's illness."], ["What is an understudy?", ["The lead actor who gets the biggest part and the most lines in the play", "Someone who learns a part in case the actor can't perform", "The play's director", "A person who builds sets"], 1, "\"She memorized the whole part and then, almost certainly, never performed it.\""], ["What does the phrase 'the thrill curdled into guilt' mean?", ["She felt sick from eating too much cheese before rehearsal", "Her excitement turned sour and became guilt", "She became more excited", "She stopped feeling anything"], 1, "Curdled suggests something good going bad, like milk souring."], ["How does Priya show she supports Maya?", ["She asks the director to cancel the play until she is completely well", "She takes the role back", "She texts tips, sends flowers and cheers from the audience", "She stays home and doesn't watch"], 2, "Priya sends reminders, a bouquet with a card, and holds up a sign at curtain call."], ["Which detail shows that Maya made the role her own?", ["She wore Priya's old costume, which had been fitted just for her friend", "She stood where Priya stood", "She paused in new places and laughed at a serious line", "Her hands shook at first"], 2, "\"She did not play Juliet exactly the way Priya had.\""]]},{"id": "r7_x4", "lv": 7, "e": "⛰️", "t": "The Turnaround Time", "p": ["At four in the morning, the trailhead parking lot was dark except for the bobbing circles of headlamps. Nico zipped his jacket to his chin and followed his aunt Rosa up the rocky path. Their goal was the summit of Mount Carlin, a peak that rose more than twelve thousand feet into the thin Colorado air.", "Before they started, Aunt Rosa had set a rule. 'Our turnaround time is noon,' she said. 'Wherever we are at twelve o'clock, we head down. No arguments.' In the mountains, she explained, summer storms often build in the early afternoon. Being above the tree line during a lightning storm is one of the most dangerous places a hiker can be, because you become the tallest thing around.", "The climb was harder than Nico expected. The air was so thin that he had to stop every few minutes to catch his breath. By eleven, they had climbed above the trees onto a ridge of broken rock, and the summit looked close, maybe forty-five minutes away. But clouds were piling up to the west, their bottoms turning gray.", "At 11:55, Aunt Rosa checked her watch and stopped. Nico stared at the summit. 'We're so close,' he said. 'We could make it and be back down in no time.' His aunt looked at the clouds, then at him. 'That's what every hiker who gets into trouble up here says,' she answered gently. 'The mountain will still be here next weekend. We need to be too.'", "Nico wanted to argue, but he turned around. They were back among the trees by one o'clock when the first thunder boomed across the valley, and within minutes the ridge they had stood on disappeared into a gray curtain of hail and lightning.", "Nico watched it from the shelter of the pines. He had not reached the summit, but for the first time he understood that a good decision is not always the one that feels good in the moment."], "q": [["What is the theme of this story?", ["Mountains are far too dangerous for anyone to ever try to climb", "Always climb faster than your group", "A wise decision sometimes means giving up what you want", "Hail is worse than lightning"], 2, "Nico turns back short of the summit, and the storm proves it was the right choice."], ["Why did Aunt Rosa set a turnaround time before the hike?", ["Afternoon storms often build in the mountains", "She wanted to be home in time for a big family lunch", "The trail closed at noon", "Nico was a slow hiker"], 0, "\"Summer storms often build in the early afternoon.\""], ["Why is being above the tree line during a lightning storm so dangerous?", ["A hiker becomes the tallest thing around", "The rocks up there get very slippery when wet", "There is no water there", "The air is too thin to breathe"], 0, "\"You become the tallest thing around.\""], ["What does Aunt Rosa mean by 'The mountain will still be here next weekend. We need to be too'?", ["They should come back when it is safe", "The mountain might fall down in the storm", "She is tired of hiking", "They are going to camp there"], 0, "She means safety matters more than reaching the top today; they can try another time."], ["Which event confirms that turning around was the right choice?", ["Nico finally caught his breath after resting on a rock for a while", "They reached the trees at one", "The sun rose at dawn", "A storm of hail and lightning hit the ridge soon after"], 3, "\"Within minutes the ridge they had stood on disappeared into a gray curtain of hail and lightning.\""]]});
